on evidence/support | §2.1.1 reviewed R3 cost source in this lesson base, Book 1 §1.1.3 table-to-line/scale instruction and §1.3.2 equality solving/checking are actual curricular evidence, not learner-mastery evidence. Retrieval is printed before dependent use and repeated in Start 1; worked example and optional guided route provide support. No prior formal break-even or prior revenue mastery is assumed |\n| Explicit non-goals | MK/MO, MO=MK, formal output-choice optimization, calculus/quadratic solving, cost-function classification, elasticity, surplus, market supply/demand, profit as an area; no target/MTU/registry repair |\n| Downstream prepares-for | §2.1.3 interval changes in costs/revenue; §2.1.4 integrated profit reasoning; revenue comparison in §2.2.2. Do not front-load these later methods |\n| Retrieval | Only substitution, division/units, linear equality and plotting/scaling required by this target and worked example |\n| Interleaving | TK and GTK from §2.1.1 in Start 1 and one closing retrieval task, to distinguish money per period from money per product; no untaught marginal calculation |\n| Operation emphasis | Form a function; calculate totals/averages and signed profit; explain conditional cancellation; solve/check an equality; distinguish continuous/discrete outputs; independently construct and interpret a labelled two-line graph |\n| Misconception boundary | TO is not profit; GO is revenue per sold unit, not total revenue or profit; GO=P requires constant unit price and Q>0; no-loss integer output is not conventional rounding; monetary profit is a vertical distance, never a filled surface here |\n| Model conditions/relevant range | One enterprise and one stated period, constant price per sold unit, Q sold equals Q produced/served, fixed cost plus constant variable cost per unit within the stated range. No empirical claim outside the supplied model; Q=0 permitted for totals but not averages |\n\nThe §2.1.1 bakery capacity restriction is not silently 