same Q; graphical interpretation taught progressively | G3 explain annotated crossing/zones; G4 mark without labels supplied | I6d mark (13⅓,53⅓) and zones | Covered; algebra and graph shown as same relationship |\n| 4: vertical profit distance | d: same Q, subtract money coordinates, not area | WE6 Q6:42−26=16 vertical bracket | Start2 signed difference, no new graph production before teaching | G3 explain vertical Q6 gap8; G4 draw Q8 gap9; G5 distinguish revenue/profit | I6d Q20 gap20 | Covered; unsupported I6 before T7; no filled surfaces |\n\n### Exercise, explanation, and worked-example sequence\n\n#### Instruction design and conditions\n\nOpen with a small theatre evening: at €5 per visitor, a full cash box does not\nyet tell the organizer whether costs are covered. State one evening, Q paying\nvisitors (0–30), TK=60+2Q, each ticket €5, all visits paid, constant model over\nthat range. Use the same case throughout theory to avoid extraneous context\nswitches. State that function drawings are continuous models although actual\nvisitors are whole people.\n\nBefore new dependent operations, print a compact recall box: substitute Q into\nTK, distinguish € per evening from € per visitor, divide by positive Q,\nmaintain equality when moving terms, plot two coordinates on uniformly spaced\naxes. Reference the actual earlier paragraphs, without claiming secure mastery.\nWorked reminders are present in print even when optional exercises are skipped.\n\n| Theory step | Teaching move and dual coding | Checked anchor |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Define total revenue/omzet, TO=P×Q; verbal sale → short two-column table → TO line | Q0/10/20/30 gives TO0/50/100/150; fig1 |\n| 2 | Define average revenue GO=TO/Q, Q>0; show cancellation beside table column €/visitor | Q10 gives50/10=5; Q20 gives100/20=5; GO at0 undefined; do not add a per-unit line to total-money axes |\n| 3 | Define profit=TO−TK, negative means loss; add TK to same graph and compare numeric totals | TK60/80/100/120;