WE is unnumbered.\n\n1. `## Uitgewerkt voorbeeld`\n2. `## Startopgaven`\n3. `## Begeleide inoefening`\n4. `## Zelfstandige oefening`\n5. `## Doeloefening`\n6. `## Denkertje / Bonusopgave`\n7. `## Herhaling / Herhaling en interleaving`\n\n| Stage / item | Planned concrete task, source and answer anchors | Support / time |\n|---|---|---|\n| Worked example WE1–6 | Kayak rentals, one day, Q0–6 rentals, TK=20+Q, fixed price€7/rental. TO7Q; GO7 for Q>0. Q2:14−22=−8; Q6:42−26=16. Solve7Q=20+Q →6Q20→10/3≈3,33; first whole4, profit4; at3 profit−2. Demonstrate graph axes Q rentals/day and money€/day; TK(0,20),(6,26), TO(0,0),(6,42), crossing(10/3,70/3), zones and vertical16 atQ6. Select readable scales explicitly and show plotting method, not merely finished result | Complete same target chain, simpler numbers; fig we1; 8min |\n| Compact recap box | At most five points: TO/GO and conditions; profit difference/sign; equality and whole-unit check; joint graph crossing/zones; same-Q vertical gap, never area. Brief forward reference to §2.1.3 comparing changes, with no new formula. Include self-check wording linked to the four goals | Non-heading immediately after WE; included in transitions budget |\n| Start1 retrieval | Given a candle workshop TK=12+3Q for Q0–8 candles in one afternoon, calculate TK at2=18 and GTK=9€/candle; distinguish units. Identify cost coordinates(0,12),(2,18), explain uniform axis spacing. Solve the prior-maths equality3Q=12+Q →Q6 and check18=18; do not call it break-even in this retrieval task | Earlier §211/Book1 operations, four compact parts; 4min |\n| Start2 comprehension | Same workshop sells2 candles at fixed€8 each. TO16, GO8, profit16−18=−2€/afternoon. Explain why “€16 received means €16 profit” fails and why GO is not16. This checks current meaning, not a score-based mastery decision | Two linked brief parts; 2min |\n| Guided3 | Soap sales/day, Q0–6 bars, TK=10+Q, P4. Supply fully annotated worked lines, graph and algebra; pupil completes TO at2