      "stdout": "# Course Blueprint v6 Three-Year Umbrella\n\nStatus: owned three-year umbrella planning end-state authority\nVersion: v6-three-year umbrella\nDetailed Year 1 baseline: `references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md`\nPromoted by: `BLUEPRINT-V6-AUTHORITY-PROMOTION-1`\nMaturity packet: `THREE-YEAR-BLUEPRINT-MATURITY-BUNDLE-1`\nDo not use for protected reference mutation, product-route adoption, or\nstudent-facing authority.\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis owned source defines the intended mature course shape: an 11-book,\nthree-year, exam-grounded curriculum contract. It preserves v5 as the detailed\nYear 1 / Books 1-4 baseline and supplies the book-level Year 2 and Year 3\numbrella so later work can be reviewed against one coherent route.\n\nThis file does not mint paragraphs, MTUs, target exercises, exam-operation\nrecords, product routes, or student-facing lessons. It is an owned umbrella\nplanning authority that reconciles:\n\n- v4 as historical design evidence;\n- v5 as the active four-book / four-test-week Year 1 source;\n- `knowledge/three Year blue print.md` as rough later-year concept input;\n- the product vision and product end-state;\n- the reference roadmap's exam-ingestion north star.\n\n## End-State Statement\n\nThe mature 4veco course blueprint is an 11-book, three-year exam-training spine. Every formal test week maps to one book, and every book maps to a cumulative path toward VWO economics exam capability.\n\nEach exam-relevant concept, calculation, graph/table operation, source-reading operation, reasoning form, and answer-writing form must be introduced, practised, consolidated, retrieved, and finally tested in CvTE-style contexts.\n\n## Authority Boundary\n\nThis source is owned curriculum-design prose. It is not external authority and\nit is not a machine registry.\n\nAuthority depends on the claim type:\n\n| Claim type | Highest authority | v6 use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Official exam demand | Real CvTE exam question, source annex, and official correction model | Determines required operations, answer forms, scoring logic, and exam anchors. |\n| Target-exercise quality | Reviewed target exercise or authored exercise record | Determines paragraph target, cognitive level, and operation chain. |\n| Book placement and assessment calendar | Active owned blueprint | v6 is the umbrella placement source; v5 remains detailed Year 1 source until explicit migration. |\n| MTU identity, dependencies, terms, and status | `references/machine/*` through CLI governance | Blueprint prose may map to MTUs but cannot override registry facts or mutate them. |\n| Exercise operation metadata | Reviewed exercise-operation overlays and later promoted registries | v6 can name needed operation rows but not promote provisional operation labels. |\n| Coverage grouping | Syllabus/eindtermen | Use for orientation and gap checks, not automatic unit creation. |\n| Drift, gaps, and current status | Generated reports and sprint records | Use as diagnostics and audit context, not primary authority when source records exist. |\n| Rough future sequencing | Planning notes such as `knowledge/three Year blue print.md` | Use as input only after reconciliation against active sources and registries. |\n\n## Core Decisions\n\n- The final course uses 11 books, not the older 13-book concept.\n- The formal assessment calendar is 4 + 4 + 3 books across three years.\n- Each formal test week corresponds to one book.\n- The final planning count model is 148 count-bearing lessons and 152 scheduled\n  modules/cycles overall.\n- Books 5-11 have exact planning counts: Book 5 = 13, Book 6 = 13,\n  Book 7 = 14, Book 8 = 16, Book 9 = 14, Book 10 = 15, and Book 11 =\n  9 count-bearing modules plus 4 scheduled timed-paper/repair cycles.\n- v6 is the three-year umbrella planning authority for book-level route,\n  assessment-calendar structure, and sequencing.\n- v5 remains the detailed Year 1 / Books 1-4 baseline until a later explicit\n  migration replaces or absorbs those details.\n- Books 1-10 do not regain separate printed test-preparation chapters by default; each book should carry cumulative retrieval and web-only test-preparation support unless a later reviewed book design explicitly authorizes another model.\n- Book 11 carries final exam training and cumulative integration; it is not followed by a separate final exam book.\n- Paragraph-level production waits for reviewed target exercises and\n  exam-operation evidence.\n- Machine references remain CLI-only.\n\n## Relationship To Earlier Sources\n\n| Source | Current role | Constraint |\n|---|---|---|\n| v4 blueprint | Historical design evidence | It is partial Year 1 and uses the old printed test-preparation chapter model. |\n| v5 blueprint | Detailed Year 1 source | It covers Books 1-4, 54 count-bearing records, and web-only test prep. |\n| Three-year concept | Rough planning input | Its finance/risk/macro sequencing is useful, but its 13-book load is not adopted. |\n| Product vision | Strategic trade-off baseline | The blueprint must strengthen exam-capable performance through an efficient route. |\n| Product end-state | Operational route baseline | Exam-target paragraphs must trace prompt, source, correction model, operations, answer forms, and MTU implications. |\n| Reference roadmap | Exam-ingestion and governance baseline | Do not mint units from syllabus prose; use exam evidence and reviewed target exercises. |\n\n## Three-Year Book Map\n\n| Year | Books | Role |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Year 1 | Books 1-4 | Micro foundations and first exam-style source handling. |\n| Year 2 | Books 5-8 | Institutions, finance, risk, information, cooperation, public finance, and growth foundations. |\n| Year 3 | Books 9-11 | Macro policy, open economy, cumulative policy integration, and final exam training. |\n\n## Book-Level Spine\n\n| Book | Formal test-week position | Core role | Exam-training role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Year 1, test week 1 | Foundations, demand, supply, equilibrium | Basic calculations, P-Q graph/table reading, short explanations. |\n| 2 | Year 1, test week 2 | Costs, revenue, elasticity, surplus | Calculation chains, welfare areas, source-supported reasoning. |\n| 3 | Year 1, test week 3 | Government intervention and market structures | Multi-step diagrams, policy instruments, correction-model discipline. |\n| 4 | Year 1, test week 4 | Market failure, labour market, trade basics | Cumulative micro cases, labour/trade/welfare integration. |\n| 5 | Year 2, test week 1 | Time, stock-flow, pensions, intertemporal exchange | Long-horizon reasoning, indexation, sustainability arguments. |\n| 6 | Year 2, test week 2 | Interest, bonds, housing, financial markets | Interest-rate mechanics, asset-price logic, household and market applications. |\n| 7 | Year 2, test week 3 | Risk, insurance, asymmetric information | Expected value, moral hazard, adverse selection, source-based cases. |\n| 8 | Year 2, test week 4 | Game theory, public goods, redistribution, growth/public-finance bridge | Strategic reasoning, public intervention, broader policy evaluation. |\n| 9 | Year 3, test week 1 | Keynesian macro and fiscal policy | Multiplier, output gaps, fiscal measures, macro source interpretation. |\n| 10 | Year 3, test week 2 | Monetary policy, IS-MB-GA, open economy | Policy transmission, exchange-rate channel, shock analysis. |\n| 11 | Year 3, test week 3 | Integrated policy and final exam training | Mixed CvTE-style cases, full-paper practice, answer-model and time discipline. |\n\n## Final Planning Count Model\n\nThese are the accepted planning counts for the frozen three-year baseline. They\nset the book-level production model; they do not create target-registry records,\nmint MTUs, authorize lessons, or close product/Scale gates.\n\n| Book | Count-bearing lessons/modules | Scheduled modules/cycles | Count-bearing model note |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| 1 | 12 | 12 | Fixed by active v5. |\n| 2 | 12 | 12 | Fixed by active v5. |\n| 3 | 14 | 14 | Fixed by active v5. |\n| 4 | 16 | 16 | Fixed by active v5. |\n| 5 | 13 | 13 | Pension/time/stock-flow book; `Y2-B5-P06` is absorbed/non-standalone through the Book 5/6 bridge. |\n| 6 | 13 | 13 | Housing finance, rent-market, interest, bonds, and financial-market book; `Y2-B6-P13` is deferred/non-count-bearing. |\n| 7 | 14 | 14 | Risk, insurance, asymmetric information, and expected-premium book. |\n| 8 | 16 | 16 | Strategic interaction, public goods, redistribution, and growth/public-finance bridge. |\n| 9 | 14 | 14 | Keynesian macro and fiscal-policy book. |\n| 10 | 15 | 15 | Monetary policy, IS-MB-GA, and open-economy book. |\n| 11 | 9 | 13 | `M01-M08` and `M13` are count-bearing; `M09-M12` are scheduled timed-paper/repair cycles. |\n\nTotals:\n\n- Year 1: 54 count-bearing lessons / 54 scheduled lessons.\n- Year 2: 56 count-bearing lessons / 56 scheduled lessons.\n- Year 3: 38 count-bearing modules / 42 scheduled modules/cycles.\n- Full course: 148 count-bearing lessons/modules / 152 scheduled\n  lessons/modules/cycles.\n\nBook 11 is final exam training: 9 count-bearing modules plus 4 scheduled\ntimed-paper/repair cycles. The four scheduled cycles are part of the assessment\ncalendar and student workload model, but they are not new count-bearing theory\nmodules.\n\n## Year 1 Embedded Baseline\n\nBooks 1-4 are inherited from v5:\n\n- Book 1: 12 count-bearing paragraphs.\n- Book 2: 12 count-bearing paragraphs.\n- Book 3: 14 count-bearing paragraphs.\n- Book 4: 16 count-bearing paragraphs.\n\nThe v5 decisions stay active:\n\n- no printed test-preparation chapter;\n- test preparation remains web-only;\n- gemengde-opgaven paragraphs are count-bearing consolidation records;\n- current target-registry finality is governed by\n  `references/authored/course-target-exercises.json`, not by older prose tables;\n- inflation/CPI and late macro material remain outside v5 count-bearing print scope unless a later reviewed decision changes that boundary.\n\nYear 1 / Book 1 foundation closure is complete at the authored-registry and\nevidence-planning layer through `Y1-FOUNDATION-CLOSURE-REVIEW-1`. That closure\ndoes not authorize generated lesson-output readiness, target-equivalent proof\nfor every Book 1 paragraph, completion language, CP-6, Scale Gate 1,\nproduct-route adoption, diagnostics, mastery, PV, summative use, or\nstudent/product use.\n\nFuture Year 1 work should therefore use v5 and the target registry as the\ndetailed baseline, and use v6 only as umbrella course-route context unless a\nlater explicit migration changes the boundary.\n\n## Year 2 Final Planning Scope\n\n### Book 5 - Time, Stock-Flow, Pensions, Intertemporal Exchange\n\nBook 5 builds the time layer that later finance and macro questions assume: stocks versus flows, intertemporal exchange, pension systems, indexation regimes, ageing, and sustainability reasoning.\n\nExpected operation families:\n\n- distinguish stock and flow variables;\n- reason over time horizons and generations;\n- calculate indexed, frozen, or wage-linked values when target evidence requires it;\n- use source tables to evaluate pension and sustainability claims;\n- write balanced policy explanations.\n\n### Book 6 - Interest, Bonds, Housing, Financial Markets\n\nBook 6 builds the finance bridge: nominal and real interest, bond price logic, household finance, housing, exchange-rate basics, saving balances, and financial-market applications.\n\nExpected operation families:\n\n- calculate and interpret interest-rate changes;\n- explain bond-price and interest-rate direction;\n- connect household choices to market conditions;\n- read financial tables and source claims;\n- prepare monetary-policy prerequisites.\n\n### Book 7 - Risk, Insurance, Asymmetric Information\n\nBook 7 teaches risk and information problems that make otherwise simple market models difficult: expected value, insurance pooling, adverse selection, moral hazard, principal-agent problems, transaction costs, and exchange-rate risk.\n\nExpected operation families:\n\n- compute expected value or expected loss where required;\n- identify moral hazard and adverse selection in sources;\n- explain how contracts, deductibles, premiums, and screening change incentives;\n- build source-supported answers using cause-effect chains.\n\n### Book 8 - Game Theory, Public Goods, Redistribution, Growth/Public Finance Bridge\n\nBook 8 compresses the older bridge-book idea into the final Year 2 book. It joins strategic interaction, public goods, collective action, redistribution, debt, taxation, growth, productivity, circular economy, and green GDP at book level.\n\nInternal spine:\n\n| Segment | Focus | Load control |\n|---|---|---|\n| 8A | Strategic interaction: dominant strategy, Nash, prisoner's dilemma | Keep payoff logic and equilibrium reasoning together. |\n| 8B | Collective action and public goods: free riding, intervention, welfare | Use public-good cases to retrieve welfare and intervention logic from Books 2-4. |\n| 8C | Redistribution and public finance: tax, debt, redistribution, incentives | Tie fiscal choices to incentives and distribution; avoid full macro-policy drift. |\n| 8D | Growth and sustainability bridge: productivity, green GDP, circular economy | Prepare Book 9 macro without turning Book 8 into a macro book. |\n\nExpected operation families:\n\n- identify dominant strategy and Nash-equilibrium patterns;\n- explain prisoner-dilemma and collective-good outcomes;\n- evaluate public intervention and redistribution trade-offs;\n- connect growth, sustainability, debt, and public finance to policy choices.\n\n## Year 3 Final Planning Scope\n\n### Book 9 - Keynesian Macro And Fiscal Policy\n\nBook 9 starts the macro-policy year: Keynesian demand, multiplier, output gap, automatic stabilisers, anticyclical fiscal policy, shocks, inflation channels, and labour-market channels.\n\nExpected operation families:\n\n- calculate or interpret multiplier effects where target evidence requires it;\n- identify output gaps and demand/supply shocks;\n- choose and justify fiscal measures;\n- read macro graphs, tables, and policy-source excerpts.\n\n### Book 10 - Monetary Policy, IS-MB-GA, Open Economy\n\nBook 10 covers monetary transmission, central-bank decisions, IS-MB-GA or related policy representations, exchange-rate channels, open-economy constraints, and policy trade-offs. More technical constructs such as trilemma reasoning or interest parity require explicit exam or target-exercise anchors before paragraph production.\n\nExpected operation families:\n\n- trace monetary policy transmission;\n- interpret IS-MB-GA or related macro-policy representations;\n- explain exchange-rate effects on trade, inflation, and competitiveness;\n- compare policy mixes under constraints.\n\n### Book 11 - Integrated Policy And Final Exam Training\n\nBook 11 is the cumulative exam book. It folds the older \"advanced integration\" and \"final exam training\" books into one test-week scope.\n\nInternal spine:\n\n| Segment | Focus | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| 11A | Exam operation decomposition: prompt, source, graph/table, calculation, reasoning, answer form | Students learn to unpack mixed CvTE-style prompts before solving. |\n| 11B | Mixed domain retrieval: micro, finance, risk, macro in one case | Students retrieve across Books 1-10 under realistic source pressure. |\n| 11C | Correction-model discipline: point allocation, command words, answer construction | Students practise scoring logic and answer-form precision. |\n| 11D | Timed full-paper practice: official-style papers, review, repair, retest | Students build time discipline through feedback and retest loops. |\n\nExpected operation families:\n\n- decompose mixed CvTE-style prompts into concepts, sources, calculations, graphs/tables, reasoning, and answer forms;\n- practise full correction-model answer construction;\n- retrieve micro, institution, finance, risk, information, and macro concepts in mixed cases;\n- manage time and scoring discipline across full papers.\n\n## Exam-Operation Spine\n\nThe table below is the central contract for later maturation. It is owned\nbook-level planning, not completed official exam evidence. Each row carries an\noperation id, anchor status, task-family expectation, answer-form expectation,\nand retrieval checkpoints. Rows marked `decomposition_needed` must be split\nfurther before paragraph production.\n\n| Operation id | Operation | Anchor status | First intro | First paragraph candidate | Official exam anchor needed | Target-exercise anchor needed | Shared task family | Answer form type | Retrieval checkpoints |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---|---|---|\n| OP-A1 | Basic percentage change | v5_active_needs_review | Book 1 | 1.1.2 | yes | yes | calculation_work, numeric_input | labelled calculation plus contextual sentence | Books 2, 4, 5, 9, 11 |\n| OP-A2 | Index and CPI arithmetic | v5_active_needs_review | Book 1 | 1.1.2 | yes | yes | calculation_work, formula_builder | formula, substitution, final index or percent | Books 4, 5, 9, 11 |\n| OP-A3 | Real/nominal and deflator operations | decomposition_needed | Book 4/5 | TBD | yes | yes | calculation_work, source_value_selection | calculation plus economic interpretation | Books 5, 9, 11 |\n| OP-G1 | P-Q graph reading and interpolation | v5_backfill_mapping | Book 1 | 1.1.3 | yes | yes | table_value_selection, numeric_input | source value plus reading sentence | Books 1-4, 6, 9, 11 |\n| OP-G2 | Table-to-graph construction with economist axes | v5_backfill_mapping | Book 1 | 1.1.3 | yes | yes | label_placement, point_placement_or_graph_substitute | constructed representation with labels | Books 1-4, 11 |\n| OP-G3 | Shift versus movement and before-after graph state | v5_active_needs_review | Book 1 | 1.2.2/1.3.3 | yes | yes | label_placement, sentence_builder | causal graph explanation | Books 2-4, 9-11 |\n| OP-D1 | Demand/supply equilibrium solving | v5_active_needs_review | Book 1 | 1.3.2 | yes | yes | calculation_work | equation setup, solution, unit sentence | Books 2-4, 8-11 |\n| OP-D2 | Individual-to-collective aggregation | v5_backfill_mapping | Book 1 | 1.2.3 | yes | yes | table_value_selection, calculation_work | table/function aggregation trace | Books 2, 8, 11 |\n| OP-C1 | Cost, revenue, and profit calculation | v5_active_needs_review | Book 2 | 2.1.1/2.1.2 | yes | yes | calculation_work, formula_builder | calculation chain with labels | Books 2-3, 8, 11 |\n| OP-C2 | Marginal reasoning and output choice | v5_active_needs_review | Book 2 | 2.1.3 | yes | yes | step_ordering, calculation_work | marginal rule plus decision sentence | Books 3, 8, 11 |\n| OP-E1 | Elasticity calculation and interpretation | v5_active_needs_review | Book 2 | 2.2.1 | yes | yes | calculation_work, two_tier_choice | formula, sign, classification, interpretation | Books 2-5, 8, 11 |\n| OP-W1 | Surplus and deadweight-loss area selection | v5_active_needs_review | Book 2 | 2.3.1/2.3.3 | yes | yes | label_placement, calculation_work | area identification plus welfare conclusion | Books 3-4, 8, 11 |\n| OP-P1 | Tax/subsidy/price-control mechanics | v5_active_needs_review | Book 3 | 3.1.1-3.1.5 | yes | yes | source_chain_builder, calculation_work | instrument-effect chain | Books 4, 8-11 |\n| OP-M1 | Market-structure and market-failure evaluation | v5_active_needs_review | Books 3-4 | 3.2/3.3/4.1 | yes | yes | sentence_builder, constructed_response | compare, justify, evaluate | Books 8, 11 |\n| OP-LT1 | Labour and trade market application | v5_active_needs_review | Book 4 | 4.2/4.3 | yes | yes | table_value_selection, source_chain_builder | application plus policy explanation | Books 9-11 |\n| OP-T1 | Time, stock-flow, pension sustainability | draft_candidate | Book 5 | TBD | yes | yes | source_value_selection, sentence_builder | long-horizon source-supported explanation | Books 6, 9, 11 |\n| OP-F1 | Interest, bond, housing, and finance mechanics | draft_candidate | Book 6 | TBD | yes | yes | calculation_work, source_chain_builder | direction, mechanism, application | Books 10-11 |\n| OP-R1 | Risk, insurance, and asymmetric information | draft_candidate | Book 7 | TBD | yes | yes | calculation_work, assertion_reason, constructed_response | expected value or incentive explanation | Books 8, 11 |\n| OP-S1 | Game-theory and collective-action reasoning | draft_candidate | Book 8 | TBD | yes | yes | matching_pairs, step_ordering, constructed_response | payoff logic and intervention rationale | Book 11 |\n| OP-H1 | Growth, redistribution, public-finance trade-offs | draft_candidate | Book 8 | TBD | yes | yes | source_chain_builder, constructed_response | balanced policy evaluation | Books 9, 11 |\n| OP-K1 | Keynesian macro and fiscal policy | draft_candidate | Book 9 | TBD | yes | yes | calculation_work, label_placement, source_chain_builder | macro channel plus policy choice | Book 11 |\n| OP-MP1 | Monetary transmission and open-economy channels | draft_candidate | Book 10 | TBD | yes | yes | source_chain_builder, label_placement | transmission chain and trade-off explanation | Book 11 |\n| OP-ANS1 | Command-word handling and point allocation | decomposition_needed | Book 1, deepens every book | TBD | yes | yes | step_ordering, matching_pairs | command-word to answer-plan mapping | Every book, especially Book 11 |\n| OP-ANS2 | Calculation-answer formatting | decomposition_needed | Book 1, deepens every calculation book | TBD | yes | yes | calculation_work | method, substitution, intermediate, final, unit | Every calculation-heavy book |\n| OP-ANS3 | Source-supported explanation and evaluation answer | decomposition_needed | Book 1, deepens every book | TBD | yes | yes | source_chain_builder, sentence_builder, constructed_response | claim, evidence, mechanism, conclusion | Every book, especially Book 11 |\n\n## Compression Decision: 13 Books To 11 Books\n\nDecision: the final course uses 11 books, not 13.\n\nReason: the intended formal assessment structure is 4 + 4 + 3 test weeks.\n\nConsequences:\n\n- The older Book 9 growth/public-finance bridge is folded into Book 8 and, where macro-policy evidence requires it, Book 9.\n- The older Book 12 advanced integration and Book 13 final exam training are folded into Book 11.\n- Final exam training becomes the dominant function of Book 11 and a cumulative layer running through Year 3, not a separate twelfth or thirteenth book.\n\n## Open Production Proof And Authority Boundaries\n\n- Official exam anchors per operation cluster still require reviewed\n  exam-ingestion records, source-annex traces, and correction-model traces.\n- Operation rows with `decomposition_needed` are not production-ready and must\n  be split before paragraph target-exercise work.\n- Target-exercise anchors and MTU/task-family compatibility remain production\n  blockers until governed target packets and protected CLI workflows close them.\n- v5 remains the detailed Year 1 baseline; any migration away from v5 requires a\n  separate reviewed migration path.\n- Product proof, Scale Gate 1, CP-6, diagnostics, mastery, PV, summative use,\n  and student/product use remain blocked until separate gates close or are\n  explicitly waived.\n- A09/A24/A31 collective-supply overlap needs bounded design review before\n  later-year dependency claims.\n- Q19 remains an exact HOLD only for its own source/graph reconstruction,\n  multi-market graph/PV chain, Q19-dependent MTU-H5 work, and Q19-dependent\n  lesson handoff. It does not block Book 10 architecture, q23/q24,\n  other IS-MB-GA work, or unrelated open-economy evidence ingestion.\n\n## Production Critical Path\n\nThe completed authority-promotion, operation-spine, Year 2 root mapping, and\nYear 3 macro-spine mapping work are now source context. The next production\nsequence is:\n\n1. Book 5 pension/time/stock-flow source-family target packet.\n2. Book 6 housing finance and rent-market mixed-case target packet.\n3. Book 7 credit insurance, moral hazard