n            \"prompt\": \"The price of a bicycle rises from €800 to €920. Calculate the percentage change.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"In 2023, a standard shopping basket costs €150. In 2025 the same basket costs €162. The base year is 2023 (index = 100). Calculate the price index for 2025.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"In 2026 the index rises to 112. Calculate the price increase from 2025 to 2026 in percent.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A student claims: 'The index went from 108 to 112, so inflation is 4%.' Explain why this is wrong and calculate the correct figure.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Calculate percentage changes using (new − old) / old × 100\",\n        \"Calculate and interpret index numbers\",\n        \"Distinguish between index point change and percentage change\",\n        \"Apply these skills to economic data (prices, wages, production)\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A38\",\n        \"A39\",\n        \"D31\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A38\",\n        \"A39\",\n        \"D31\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Students are weaker at percentage calculations than teachers expect. The index-point vs percentage trap (exercise d) is a classic exam mistake. This paragraph lays the foundation for ALL later calculations (elasticities, inflation, real/nominal). Worth investing time. One lesson is tight — assign exercise (d) type problems as homework.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §1.1.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"1.1.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": false,\n        \"review_completed_by\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_completed_on\": \"2026-06-14\"\n      },\n      \"review_evidence\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-14\",\n        \"review_packet\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-review-packet.md\",\n        \"quality_log\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-quality-log.md\",\n        \"sprint_plan\": \"reports/sprints/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-plan.md\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Target covers percentage change, index calculation, index-to-percent conversion, and the D31 index-point trap.\",\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Reviewed source-registry target-quality decision only; does not close Year 1, CP-6, target-equivalent proof, graph/table evidence, generated lesson output, Scale Gate authority, diagnostics, mastery, PV, product-route adoption, or student-facing product use.\"\n      },\n      \"target_quality_review\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-14\",\n        \"disposition\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"operation_chain\": [\n          \"Calculate a percentage change from old and new values.\",\n          \"Calculate an index number from a base year.\",\n          \"Calculate a percentage change between index values.\",\n          \"Explain why an index-point change is not automatically the same as a percentage change.\"\n        ],\n        \"answer_form\": [\n          \"Calculation lines using the correct base value.\",\n          \"Final percentage or index answer with notation.\",\n          \"Short explanation distinguishing index points from percent.\"\n        ],\n        \"mtu_mapping_decision\": \"A38, A39, and D31 cover the target chain; no missing MTU remains for the registry target.\",\n        \"blocks\": [],\n        \"does_not_block\": [\n          \"Separate target-equivalent lesson proof review\",\n          \"Separate Year 1 / CP-6 / Scale Gate closure review\"\n        ],\n        \"proof_required_to_close\": \"Registry validator passes and review evidence remains linked; later product proof must be handled by separate evidence lanes.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"1.1.3\",\n      \"module\": 1,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 3,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Grafieken en tabellen\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"LIGHT\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"The table below shows the number of ice creams sold at different prices: P=€1.00→Q=500, €1.50→400, €2.00→300, €2.50→200, €3.00→100.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw this as a graph with price on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal axis.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Read from your graph: how many ice creams are sold at €1.75?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A newspaper writes: 'Ice cream sales dropped by 50%.' Looking at the table, between which two prices could this have happened? Explain.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Read values from graphs and tables\",\n        \"Draw a graph from tabular data with correct axes\",\n        \"Interpolate between data points\",\n        \"Critically interpret claims using data\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A38\",\n        \"A45\",\n        \"A46\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A38\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A45\",\n        \"A46\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Skill-based, hands-on. Students enjoy the concreteness. The main pitfall is putting axes wrong (price on horizontal instead of vertical). One lesson comfortable.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §1.1.3\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"1.1.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": false,\n        \"review_completed_by\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_completed_on\": \"2026-06-14\"\n      },\n      \"review_evidence\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-14\",\n        \"review_packet\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-review-packet.md\",\n        \"quality_log\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-quality-log.md\",\n        \"sprint_plan\": \"reports/sprints/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-plan.md\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Target-registry review maps graph drawing and graph reading/interpolation to live A45/A46 units. This does not close separate graph/table lesson-evidence blockers.\",\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Reviewed source-registry target-quality decision only; does not close Year 1, CP-6, target-equivalent proof, graph/table evidence, generated lesson output, Scale Gate authority, diagnostics, mastery, PV, product-route adoption, or student-facing product use.\"\n      },\n      \"target_quality_review\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-14\",\n        \"disposition\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"operation_chain\": [\n          \"Read price-quantity data from a table.\",\n          \"Draw a P-Q graph using economist axes.\",\n          \"Interpolate a value from the graph.\",\n          \"Check a percentage claim against the table data.\"\n        ],\n        \"answer_form\": [\n          \"Graph with P on the vertical axis and Q on the horizontal axis.\",\n          \"Interpolated numeric answer from the graph.\",\n          \"Short source-based explanation for the percentage claim.\"\n        ],\n        \"mtu_mapping_decision\": \"A45 and A46 are live graph/table units, with A38 covering the percentage claim; stale missing-unit flags are cleared for the registry target only.\",\n        \"blocks\": [],\n        \"does_not_block\": [\n          \"Separate target-equivalent lesson proof review\",\n          \"Separate Year 1 / CP-6 / Scale Gate closure review\"\n        ],\n        \"proof_required_to_close\": \"Registry validator passes and review evidence remains linked; later product proof must be handled by separate evidence lanes.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"difficulty\": \"MIXED\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"A school fundraiser sells healthy lunch boxes. Students use a price-quantity table, a graph, and a campaign claim to combine scarcity, opportunity-cost, percentage, index, table-reading, graphing, interpolation, and data-claim evaluation from 1.1.1-1.1.3. The target introduces no new economic theory.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the lunch-box price-quantity table as a graph with price on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal axis: P=EUR 4 -> Q=180, P=EUR 5 -> Q=150, P=EUR 6 -> Q=120, P=EUR 7 -> Q=90.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Read or interpolate from the graph: about how many lunch boxes would be sold at EUR 5.50?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Use EUR 4 as index 100. Calculate the quantity index at EUR 6 and the percentage change in quantity sold from EUR 4 to EUR 6.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The council chooses EUR 6. Explain the opportunity cost of not choosing EUR 5 in terms of giving up 30 expected boxes sold.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A poster claims: \\\"A EUR 2 price increase halves sales.\\\" Use the table and your calculations to decide whether that claim is correct.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n          \"Graph with P on the vertical axis, Q on the horizontal axis, ordered table points, and a readable scale.\",\n          \"Numeric answers with the correct base value and one clear calculation line.\",\n          \"Short written explanations that name opportunity cost and refer to the table.\"\n        ],\n        \"short_answer_model\": [\n          \"a. Plot (Q,P) points (180,4), (150,5), (120,6), and (90,7) with a downward-sloping line through the points.\",\n          \"b. About 135 lunch boxes.\",\n          \"c. Quantity index at EUR 6 with EUR 4 as base: 120 / 180 * 100 = 66.7. Percentage change: (120 - 180) / 180 * 100 = -33.3%.\",\n          \"d. Choosing EUR 6 means giving up 30 expected boxes sold compared with EUR 5. Do not introduce revenue or profit theory.\",\n          \"e. The claim is not correct for the listed EUR 2 price increases. EUR 4 to EUR 6 falls by one-third, not half; EUR 5 to EUR 7 also does not halve sales.\"\n        ],\n        \"evidence_path\": [\n          \"reports/reference-planning/Y1-PLACEHOLDER-TARGET-REVIEW-1-review-packet.md\",\n          \"reports/reference-planning/Y1-PLACEHOLDER-TARGET-REVIEW-1-quality-log.md\",\n          \"reports/reference-planning/CP.6c-mtu-backfill-classification.md\"\n        ],\n        \"no_new_theory_rationale\": \"The task combines scarcity, opportunity cost, percentages, index numbers, table reading, graph drawing, interpolation, and claim checking. It deliberately avoids revenue, profit, elasticity, consumer surplus, and market-equilibrium theory.\",\n        \"review_disposition\": \"accepted_for_governed_registry_replacement\"\n      },\n      \"mixed_target_profile\": {\n        \"integrates_paragraphs\": [\n          \"1.1.1\",\n          \"1.1.2\",\n          \"1.1.3\"\n        ],\n        \"source_selection_required\": true,\n        \"answer_construction_required\": true,\n        \"table_or_graph_interpretation_required\": true,\n        \"no_new_theory\": true\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Consolidate scarcity and opportunity-cost reasoning with percentage, index, table, graph, interpolation, and claim-evaluation skills.\",\n        \"Select relevant price-quantity data from a table and graph.\",\n        \"Write short economic explanations that use source data without introducing revenue or profit theory.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A38\",\n        \"A39\",\n        \"A43\",\n        \"A45\",\n        \"A46\",\n        \"B01\",\n        \"B02\",\n        \"D31\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A38\",\n        \"A39\",\n        \"A43\",\n        \"A45\",\n        \"A46\",\n        \"B01\",\n        \"B02\",\n        \"D31\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Reviewed integration target accepted from PR #42. It introduces no new theory. The main difficulty is transfer: students must combine graph/table reading, interpolation, percentage/index calculation, opportunity-cost language, and source-claim evaluation without being led step by step.\",\n      \"id\": \"1.1.4\",\n      \"module\": 1,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 4,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Gemengde opgaven: economisch denken en rekenen\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"gemengde_opgaven\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": false,\n      \"record_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §1.1.4\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": null,\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": false,\n        \"review_completed_by\": \"B1-PLACEHOLDER-REGISTRY-REPLACEMENT-1\",\n        \"review_completed_on\": \"2026-06-12\"\n      },\n      \"review_evidence\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-PLACEHOLDER-REGISTRY-REPLACEMENT-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-12\",\n        \"candidate_packet\": \"reports/reference-planning/Y1-PLACEHOLDER-TARGET-REVIEW-1-review-packet.md\",\n        \"quality_log\": \"reports/reference-planning/Y1-PLACEHOLDER-TARGET-REVIEW-1-quality-log.md\",\n        \"source_pr\": \"https://github.com/meijer1973/4veco-platform/pull/42\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Accepted PR #42 candidate target for governed authored-registry replacement. Wording preserves the opportunity-cost focus as giving up 30 expected boxes sold and avoids revenue/profit theory.\",\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Reviewed source-registry replacement only; does not close Year 1, CP-6, target-equivalent proof, graph/table 1.1.3 closure, lesson output, Scale Gate authority, or student-facing product use.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"1.2.1\",\n      \"module\": 1,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 1,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Individuele vraag\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"LIGHT\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Lisa loves pizza. She is willing to pay €8 for her first pizza, €6 for the second, €4 for the third, and €1.50 for the fourth.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw Lisa's individual demand curve (step function) with price on the vertical axis.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The market price is €5. How many pizzas does Lisa buy? Explain using the concept of betalingsbereidheid.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The price drops to €3. How many does she buy now?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Explain in your own words why the demand curve slopes downward.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Define betalingsbereidheid (willingness to pay)\",\n        \"Explain the relationship between price and quantity demanded\",\n        \"Draw an individual demand curve from willingness-to-pay data\",\n        \"Explain the law of demand\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A44\",\n        \"D35\",\n        \"D36\",\n        \"D37\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A44\",\n        \"D35\",\n        \"D36\",\n        \"D37\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"D1.1\",\n        \"D1.2\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Very intuitive. Pizza/real-life examples work well. Step functions are visual and concrete. One lesson comfortable — use the extra time for a good class discussion about what determines betalingsbereidheid.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §1.2.1\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"1.2.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": false,\n        \"review_completed_by\": \"INSPECT-9A\",\n        \"review_completed_on\": \"2026-06-11\"\n      },\n      \"review_evidence\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"INSPECT-9A\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-11\",\n        \"review_report_md\": \"reports/inspection-standards/chapter-1-2-target-exam-linkage-remediation.md\",\n        \"review_report_json\": \"reports/inspection-standards/chapter-1-2-target-exam-linkage-remediation.json\",\n        \"sprint_plan\": \"archive/sprints/INSPECT-9A/INSPECT-9A-sprint-plan.md\",\n        \"planning_review\": \"archive/sprints/INSPECT-9A/INSPECT-9A-planning-review.md\",\n        \"lesson_evidence_commit\": \"b858bca602bb7afdf75cad7c3ecc1a79b31fbb76\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Existing doeloefening-aligned individual-demand target reviewed final; D3.1 remains lesson/practice evidence, not a target-exercise registry code.\",\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Reviewed source-registry target/exam-code decision only; does not close target-equivalent proof, accessibility/support evidence, pack readiness, or school-owned evidence.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"1.2.2\",\n      \"module\": 1,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 2,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Vraagfactoren\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"The market for butter is in equilibrium at P = €2 and Q = 1000.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The price of butter rises to €2.50. Show in a graph what happens to the quantity demanded. Is this a shift of the demand curve or a movement along it?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A health report claims that margarine is unhealthy. Show in a graph what happens to the demand for butter. Is this a shift or a movement?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Average income in the country rises by 5%. Butter is a normal good. Show the effect on the demand curve.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Name four factors that cause the demand curve to shift and give an example for each.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Distinguish between a movement along the demand curve (caused by own price change) and a shift of the demand curve (caused by other factors)\",\n        \"Name and explain demand factors: income, preferences, price of substitutes/complements, expectations\",\n        \"Show shifts graphically (left/right)\",\n        \"Classify goods as substitutes or complements based on context\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A42\",\n        \"D27\",\n        \"D32\",\n        \"D33\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A42\",\n        \"D27\",\n        \"D32\",\n        \"D33\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"D1.4b\",\n        \"D1.9\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"The shift vs movement distinction is the single most common error in all of microeconomics. Students confuse these for months. The lesson must hammer this with at least 4–5 examples. One lesson is tight but essential — this concept returns in every subsequent chapter.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §1.2.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"1.2.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": false,\n        \"review_completed_by\": \"INSPECT-9A\",\n        \"review_completed_on\": \"2026-06-11\"\n      },\n      \"review_evidence\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"INSPECT-9A\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-11\",\n        \"review_report_md\": \"reports/inspection-standards/chapter-1-2-target-exam-linkage-remediation.md\",\n        \"review_report_json\": \"reports/inspection-standards/chapter-1-2-target-exam-linkage-remediation.json\",\n        \"sprint_plan\": \"archive/sprints/INSPECT-9A/INSPECT-9A-sprint-plan.md\",\n        \"planning_review\": \"archive/sprints/INSPECT-9A/INSPECT-9A-planning-review.md\",\n        \"lesson_evidence_commit\": \"b858bca602bb7afdf75cad7c3ecc1a79b31fbb76\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Existing doeloefening-aligned demand-factor target reviewed final; D1.9 confirmed and D1.4b added; D1.4a and A2.15 deferred.\",\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Reviewed source-registry target/exam-code decision only; does not close target-equivalent proof, accessibility/support evidence, pack readiness, or school-owned evidence.\"\n      },\n      \"terminology_decision\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-NORMAL-INFERIOR-TERM-DECISION-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-16\",\n        \"decision\": \"keep_year1_target_term_light\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Year 1 1.2.2 may use supplied normal-good context for an income-driven demand-shift task, but students are not required to classify normal versus inferior goods as an active Year 1 target operation.\",\n        \"target_operation_boundary\": \"Given a stated income change and supplied good type where needed, students determine the direction of the demand-curve shift and distinguish it from an own-price movement. Normal-vs-inferior classification and income-elasticity interpretation stay outside Year 1 target requirements.\",\n        \"mixed_target_boundary\": \"1.2.4 remains term-free and may consolidate income-driven demand shifts without requiring normal/inferior-good terminology.\",\n        \"evidence\": {\n          \"review_packet\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-NORMAL-INFERIOR-TERM-DECISION-1-review-packet.md\",\n          \"quality_log\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-NORMAL-INFERIOR-TERM-DECISION-1-quality-log.md\",\n          \"sprint_plan\": \"reports/sprints/B1-NORMAL-INFERIOR-TERM-DECISION-1-plan.md\"\n        },\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Authored target-registry terminology decision only; does not close target-equivalent lesson proof, Year 1, CP-6, Scale Gate, product-route adoption, diagnostics, mastery, PV, or student/product-use authority.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"1.2.3\",\n      \"module\": 1,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 3,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Van individuele naar collectieve vraag\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"There are only two consumers on the market for lemonade: Anna and Ben. Anna's Qd: P=€1→5, €2→3, €3→1, €4→0. Ben's Qd: P=€1→8, €2→5, €3→2, €4→1.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the collective demand at each price.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the individual demand curves and the collective demand curve in the same graph.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Anna's demand function is Qd = −2P + 7, Ben's is Qd = −3P + 11. Derive the collective demand function algebraically.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"At which price does Anna leave the market entirely? What happens to the collective demand curve at that point?\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Explain that collective demand is the horizontal sum of individual demand curves\",\n        \"Calculate collective demand from individual data by adding quantities at the same price\",\n        \"Derive collective demand algebraically by adding linear demand functions at the same price\",\n        \"Explain in ordinary language when one buyer group contributes zero demand beyond a price range\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A01\",\n        \"A47\",\n        \"A48\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A01\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A47\",\n        \"A48\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.9\",\n        \"D1.3\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"The horizontal addition (adding quantities, not prices) is counter-intuitive for some students. The algebraic derivation requires comfort with linear equations. Buyer-group dropout may be retained as a term-light table/function interpretation, but formal kink terminology, piecewise notation, and advanced domain analysis are not Year 1 requirements.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §1.2.3\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"1.2.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": false,\n        \"review_completed_by\": \"INSPECT-9A\",\n        \"review_completed_on\": \"2026-06-11\"\n      },\n      \"review_evidence\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"INSPECT-9A\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-11\",\n        \"review_report_md\": \"reports/inspection-standards/chapter-1-2-target-exam-linkage-remediation.md\",\n        \"review_report_json\": \"reports/inspection-standards/chapter-1-2-target-exam-linkage-remediation.json\",\n        \"sprint_plan\": \"archive/sprints/INSPECT-9A/INSPECT-9A-sprint-plan.md\",\n        \"planning_review\": \"archive/sprints/INSPECT-9A/INSPECT-9A-planning-review.md\",\n        \"lesson_evidence_commit\": \"b858bca602bb7afdf75cad7c3ecc1a79b31fbb76\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Existing doeloefening-aligned collective-demand target reviewed final; A2.9 confirmed and D1.3 added.\",\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Reviewed source-registry target/exam-code decision only; does not close target-equivalent proof, accessibility/support evidence, pack readiness, or school-owned evidence.\"\n      },\n      \"collective_demand_dropout_decision\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-COLLECTIVE-DEMAND-KINK-DISPOSITION-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-17\",\n        \"decision\": \"retain_term_light_table_based_dropout\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Retain buyer-group dropout/zero-demand reasoning as part of Year 1 collective-demand aggregation, but keep it table/function based and term-light.\",\n        \"allowed_year1_operation\": \"Given individual or group demand tables/functions, students aggregate quantities at the same price and notice/explain that a buyer group may contribute zero demand beyond a price range, changing the collective-demand shape.\",\n        \"not_required\": [\n          \"formal kink terminology\",\n          \"piecewise-function notation\",\n          \"advanced function-domain analysis\",\n          \"abstract curve-shape theory\"\n        ],\n        \"mapped_units\": [\n          \"A47\",\n          \"A48\"\n        ],\n        \"target_operation_boundary\": \"Students may explain the consumer-exit/dropout point in ordinary language as one group leaving the market or contributing zero units. They are not required to use formal kink/piecewise terminology or prove curve domains algebraically.\",\n        \"evidence\": {\n          \"review_packet\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-COLLECTIVE-DEMAND-KINK-DISPOSITION-1-review-packet.md\",\n          \"quality_log\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-COLLECTIVE-DEMAND-KINK-DISPOSITION-1-quality-log.md\",\n          \"sprint_plan\": \"reports/sprints/B1-COLLECTIVE-DEMAND-KINK-DISPOSITION-1-plan.md\",\n          \"mixed_target_audit_rerun\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-COLLECTIVE-DEMAND-KINK-DISPOSITION-1-mixed-target-audit-rerun.md\"\n        },\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Authored target-registry disposition only; does not close target-equivalent lesson proof, Year 1, CP-6, Scale Gate, product-route adoption, diagnostics, mastery, PV, or student/product-use authority.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"difficulty\": \"MIXED\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Integrated practice based on the existing generated 1.2.4 consolidation exercises: Smoothiebar Blend and IJsjes op het strand. The target consolidates individual demand, collective demand, movement versus shift, demand factors, substitute reasoning, graph reading, and function/table work without introducing new theory.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Smoothiebar Blend: calculate collective demand at P = EUR 3 by adding student and office-worker quantities at the same price.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Classify a smoothie price rise from EUR 3 to EUR 4 as movement along the demand curve or demand-curve shift, and justify the answer.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Explain why a viral preference change shifts student demand to the right and name the demand factor.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"After the preference change, derive the collective demand function while both groups are active.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Find the price where office workers leave the smoothie market and explain how the collective demand curve changes shape at that point.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"f\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Diagnose why more units sold does not always mean that the demand curve shifted.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"g\",\n            \"prompt\": \"IJsjes op het strand: read an individual quantity from the graph at P = EUR 2.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"h\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Read maximum willingness to pay from an individual demand curve and explain the price-axis intercept.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"i\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate collective demand at P = EUR 2 and P = EUR 4.50, accounting for a consumer who leaves the market.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"j\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Separate the own-price rise from the frozen-yoghurt opening and classify each as movement or shift.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"k\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Classify frozen yoghurt as substitute or complement using the context and name the demand-factor effect.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"l\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Evaluate a seller claim that hotter weather and higher demand automatically justify a price increase.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"mixed_target_profile\": {\n        \"integrates_paragraphs\": [\n          \"1.2.1\",\n          \"1.2.2\",\n          \"1.2.3\"\n        ],\n        \"source_selection_required\": true,\n        \"answer_construction_required\": true,\n        \"table_or_graph_interpretation_required\": true,\n        \"no_new_theory\": true\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Consolidate individual demand, willingness-to-pay, and buy/no-buy reasoning in mixed contexts.\",\n        \"Determine collective demand from tables, functions, and graphs by adding quantities at the same price.\",\n        \"Distinguish movement along a demand curve from demand-curve shifts in simultaneous-change contexts.\",\n        \"Classify demand-factor effects, including preference changes and substitute/complement reasoning.\",\n        \"Interpret buyer-group dropout and zero-demand effects in collective demand using tables/functions.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A01\",\n        \"A42\",\n        \"A44\",\n        \"A47\",\n        \"A48\",\n        \"D27\",\n        \"D32\",\n        \"D33\",\n        \"D35\",\n        \"D36\",\n        \"D37\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A01\",\n        \"A42\",\n        \"A44\",\n        \"A47\",\n        \"A48\",\n        \"D27\",\n        \"D32\",\n        \"D33\",\n        \"D35\",\n        \"D36\",\n        \"D37\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.9\",\n        \"D1.1\",\n        \"D1.2\",\n        \"D1.3\",\n        \"D1.4b\",\n        \"D1.9\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Reviewed integration target based on existing generated consolidation exercises and review evidence. Local generated-output flags remain visible: frozen-yoghurt wording should avoid overclaiming a price-substitute effect, and the chapter quality-ref records an orphaned asset counterpart.\",\n      \"id\": \"1.2.4\",\n      \"module\": 1,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 4,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Gemengde opgaven: vraag\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"gemengde_opgaven\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": false,\n      \"record_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §1.2.4\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": null,\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": false,\n        \"review_completed_by\": \"INSPECT-9A\",\n        \"review_completed_on\": \"2026-06-11\"\n      },\n      \"review_evidence\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"INSPECT-9A\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-11\",\n        \"review_report_md\": \"reports/inspection-standards/chapter-1-2-target-exam-linkage-remediation.md\",\n        \"review_report_json\": \"reports/inspection-standards/chapter-1-2-target-exam-linkage-remediation.json\",\n        \"sprint_plan\": \"archive/sprints/INSPECT-9A/INSPECT-9A-sprint-plan.md\",\n        \"planning_review\": \"archive/sprints/INSPECT-9A/INSPECT-9A-planning-review.md\",\n        \"lesson_evidence_commit\": \"b858bca602bb7afdf75cad7c3ecc1a79b31fbb76\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Existing generated 1.2.4 consolidation evidence used to replace placeholder with reviewed source-registry integration target; D1.4a deferred.\",\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Reviewed source-registry target/exam-code decision only; does not close target-equivalent proof, accessibility/support evidence, pack readiness, or school-owned evidence.\"\n      },\n      \"collective_demand_dropout_boundary\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-COLLECTIVE-DEMAND-KINK-DISPOSITION-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-17\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"1.2.4 may consolidate buyer-group dropout/zero-demand reasoning from 1.2.3 only within the term-light table/function boundary accepted by this sprint.\",\n        \"allowed_in_mixed_target\": \"Mixed practice may ask students to find the price where a buyer group leaves the market and explain the resulting collective-demand shape change in ordinary language.\",\n        \"not_required_in_mixed_target\": [\n          \"formal kink terminology\",\n          \"piecewise-function notation\",\n          \"advanced function-domain analysis\",\n          \"abstract curve-shape theory\"\n        ],\n        \"mapped_units\": [\n          \"A47\",\n          \"A48\"\n        ],\n        \"evidence\": {\n          \"review_packet\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-COLLECTIVE-DEMAND-KINK-DISPOSITION-1-review-packet.md\",\n          \"quality_log\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-COLLECTIVE-DEMAND-KINK-DISPOSITION-1-quality-log.md\",\n          \"sprint_plan\": \"reports/sprints/B1-COLLECTIVE-DEMAND-KINK-DISPOSITION-1-plan.md\",\n          \"mixed_target_audit_rerun\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-COLLECTIVE-DEMAND-KINK-DISPOSITION-1-mixed-target-audit-rerun.md\"\n        },\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Authored target-registry disposition only; does not close target-equivalent lesson proof, Year 1, CP-6, Scale Gate, product-route adoption, diagnostics, mastery, PV, or student/product-use authority.\"\n      },\n      \"mixed_target_audit_after_disposition\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-COLLECTIVE-DEMAND-KINK-DISPOSITION-1\",\n        \"result\": \"passes_for_registry_mixed_target_audit\",\n        \"basis\": \"The former 1.2.4 blocker is closed for authored-registry purposes because 1.2.3 now records a term-light buyer-group dropout boundary mapped to A47/A48, and 1.2.4 no longer requires formal kink terminology.\",\n        \"does_not_authorize\": [\n          \"target-equivalent lesson proof\",\n          \"Year 1 closure\",\n          \"CP-6 closure\",\n          \"Scale Gate\",\n          \"product-route adoption\",\n          \"diagnostics\",\n          \"mastery\",\n          \"PV\",\n          \"student/product use\"\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"1.3.1\",\n      \"module\": 1,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 1,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Aanbod\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Supply-side mirror of §1.2.2: how own-price vs other factors move a supply curve.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A manufacturer of solar panels faces higher silicon prices. Show in a graph what happens to the supply curve for solar panels. Explain.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The government introduces a subsidy for solar panel production. Show the effect on the supply curve.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Name three factors that cause the supply curve to shift and give an example for each.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw a supply curve. Mark a movement along the curve and a shift of the curve. Label clearly which is which.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Draw and interpret a supply curve (upward sloping)\",\n        \"Name supply factors: input prices, technology, number of suppliers, expectations, government policy\",\n        \"Distinguish between shifts and movements along the supply curve\",\n        \"Show supply shifts graphically\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A42\",\n        \"A49\",\n        \"D13\",\n        \"D32\",\n        \"D33\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A42\",\n        \"D32\",\n        \"D33\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A49\",\n        \"D13\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"D1.12\",\n        \"D1.24\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Mirror of §1.2.2. Students pick this up faster because they have the demand framework. The shift/movement distinction gets reinforced. Exercise (c) deliberately does not give away the answer about own price — if a student lists 'own price' as a shift factor, it reveals they have not grasped the distinction between movement and shift. This diagnostic value is important. One lesson comfortable.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §1.3.1\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"1.3.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": false,\n        \"review_completed_by\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_completed_on\": \"2026-06-14\"\n      },\n      \"review_evidence\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-14\",\n        \"review_packet\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-review-packet.md\",\n        \"quality_log\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-quality-log.md\",\n        \"sprint_plan\": \"reports/sprints/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-plan.md\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Target covers supply-curve drawing, movement-versus-shift reasoning, supply factors, and supply-shift explanation using live A49/A42/D13/D32/D33 mappings.\",\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Reviewed source-registry target-quality decision only; does not close Year 1, CP-6, target-equivalent proof, graph/table evidence, generated lesson output, Scale Gate authority, diagnostics, mastery, PV, product-route adoption, or student-facing product use.\"\n      },\n      \"target_quality_review\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-14\",\n        \"disposition\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"operation_chain\": [\n          \"Identify whether a supply-side event changes supply or quantity supplied.\",\n          \"Draw or shift the supply curve using economist axes.\",\n          \"Explain input-cost and subsidy effects on supply.\",\n          \"Name supply factors and distinguish shifts from movements.\"\n        ],\n        \"answer_form\": [\n          \"Graphical curve-shift answers with labelled before/after curves.\",\n          \"Short written explanations naming the relevant supply factor.\"\n        ],\n        \"mtu_mapping_decision\": \"A49 is live for supply-curve drawing, A42/D32 cover graphical shift versus movement, D33 covers shift factors, and D13 covers cost-driven supply shifts.\",\n        \"blocks\": [],\n        \"does_not_block\": [\n          \"Separate target-equivalent lesson proof review\",\n          \"Separate Year 1 / CP-6 / Scale Gate closure review\"\n        ],\n        \"proof_required_to_close\": \"Registry validator passes and review evidence remains linked; later product proof must be handled by separate evidence lanes.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"1.3.2\",\n      \"module\": 1,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 2,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Marktevenwicht\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"On the market for notebooks: Qv = −2P + 100 and Qa = 3P − 25.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the equilibrium price and equilibrium quantity.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Check your answer by substituting back into both equations.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw both curves in a graph and mark the equilibrium point.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"At a price of €30, is there a surplus (aanbodoverschot) or a shortage (vraagoverschot)? Calculate its size.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Set Qa = Qv and solve for P (equilibrium price)\",\n        \"Substitute back to find equilibrium quantity\",\n        \"Draw demand and supply curves and locate equilibrium graphically\",\n        \"Determine surplus or shortage at a given price\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A02\",\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A49\",\n        \"A51\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A02\",\n        \"A49\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A51\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.12\",\n        \"A2.15\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"First real algebraic challenge. Students who struggle with rearranging equations need support. Exercise (b) — checking the answer — builds good habits. Exercise (d) bridges to government intervention (Module 2). One lesson is tight if algebra is weak — provide a worked example first, then let students practice.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §1.3.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"1.4.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": false,\n        \"review_completed_by\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_completed_on\": \"2026-06-14\"\n      },\n      \"review_evidence\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-14\",\n        \"review_packet\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-review-packet.md\",\n        \"quality_log\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-quality-log.md\",\n        \"sprint_plan\": \"reports/sprints/B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1-plan.md\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Target covers equilibrium calculation, substitution check, graphing demand/supply curves, and surplus/shortage calculation using live A02/A04/A06/A49/A51 mappings.\",\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Reviewed source-registry target-quality decision only; does not close Year 1, CP-6, target-equivalent proof, graph/table evidence, generated lesson output, Scale Gate authority, diagnostics, mastery, PV, product-route adoption, or student-facing product use.\"\n      },\n      \"target_quality_review\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-MIGRATED-V5-TARGET-QUALITY-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-14\",\n        \"disposition\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"operation_chain\": [\n          \"Set demand equal to supply and solve for equilibrium price.\",\n          \"Substitute the price back to find and check equilibrium quantity.\",\n          \"Draw demand and supply curves and mark equilibrium.\",\n          \"Compare demanded and supplied quantities at a non-equilibrium price.\"\n        ],\n        \"answer_form\": [\n          \"Algebraic calculation with substitution check.\",\n          \"Graphical answer with labelled equilibrium point.\",\n          \"Surplus/shortage answer naming which side is larger and by how much.\"\n        ],\n        \"mtu_mapping_decision\": \"A51 is live for surplus/shortage at a non-equilibrium price, while A02/A04/A06 cover the algebra and A49 covers market-curve graphing.\",\n        \"blocks\": [],\n        \"does_not_block\": [\n          \"Separate target-equivalent lesson proof review\",\n          \"Separate Year 1 / CP-6 / Scale Gate closure review\"\n        ],\n        \"proof_required_to_close\": \"Registry validator passes and review evidence remains linked; later product proof must be handled by separate evidence lanes.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"1.3.3\",\n      \"module\": 1,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 3,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Verschuivingen en nieuw evenwicht\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Starting from the notebook market (Qv = −2P + 100, Qa = 3P − 25, equilibrium P* = 25, Q* = 50).\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A new factory opens. The new supply function is Qa = 3P − 10. Calculate the new equilibrium price and quantity.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Compare with the old equilibrium: what happened to price? To quantity? Is this logical?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Now suppose student income rises. The new demand function is Qv = −2P + 120 (supply back to original). Calculate the new equilibrium.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw a graph showing both changes (one at a time). Use arrows to show the direction of shifts.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Now suppose BOTH changes happen at the same time: supply shifts to Qa = 3P − 10 AND demand shifts to Qv = −2P + 120. Calculate the new equilibrium. What happened to quantity? What happened to price? Could you have predicted the direction of the price change without calculating? Explain.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Calculate a new equilibrium after a supply or demand shift\",\n        \"Compare old and new equilibrium: predict direction of price and quantity change\",\n        \"Connect shifts to the demand/supply factors from §1.2 and §1.3\",\n        \"Show shifts graphically with clear before/after\",\n        \"Analyse simultaneous shifts: quantity effect is clear (both increase Q), but price effect is ambiguous (one pushes up, the other pushes down)\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A02\",\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A42\",\n        \"A49\",\n        \"D13\",\n        \"D32\",\n        \"D33\",\n        \"D47\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A02\",\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A42\",\n        \"A49\",\n        \"D13\",\n        \"D32\",\n        \"D33\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"D47\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.12\",\n        \"A2.15\",\n        \"D1.4b\",\n        \"D1.12\",\n        \"D1.24\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Exercise (e) is the key diagnostic question. When both curves shift right, quantity unambiguously increases, but the price change depends on the relative size of the shifts — it could go up, down, or stay the same. Students who understand the model grasp this; students who are just plugging numbers are surprised. This is an important conceptual checkpoint. One lesson works — the algebra is revision from §1.4.1, the challenge is the reasoning in (e).\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §1.3.3\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"1.4.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": false,\n        \"review_completed_by\": \"B1-SIMSHIFT-PROTECTED-REFERENCE-IMPLEMENTATION-1\",\n        \"review_completed_on\": \"2026-06-15\"\n      },\n      \"target_quality_review\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-SIMSHIFT-PROTECTED-REFERENCE-IMPLEMENTATION-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-15\",\n        \"disposition\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"The one-shift equilibrium, graphing, and shift-factor chain remains mapped to live units, and new D47 covers simultaneous demand/supply shift reasoning with determinate quantity and ambiguous price direction without relative magnitudes.\",\n        \"operation_chain\": [\n          \"Calculate a new equilibrium after a supply shift.\",\n          \"Compare old and new equilibrium after one supply shift.\",\n          \"Calculate a new equilibrium after a demand shift.\",\n          \"Graph one-shift changes with arrows.\",\n          \"Analyze demand and supply shifts in isolation before combining them.\",\n          \"Reason that when demand and supply both shift right, equilibrium quantity rises while equilibrium price is ambiguous without relative magnitudes.\",\n          \"Use the combined-equilibrium calculation as confirmation when equations are provided.\"\n        ],\n        \"answer_form\": [\n          \"Algebraic new-equilibrium calculations with substitution checks where functions are given.\",\n          \"Graphical before/after shift representation with labelled curves and arrows.\",\n          \"Conceptual explanation that separates isolated effects, combined directional pressure, determinate quantity, and ambiguous price.\"\n        ],\n        \"mtu_mapping_decision\": \"A02/A04/A06 cover algebraic equilibrium calculation, A42/A49 cover graphical shift representation, D13/D32/D33 cover one-shift and shift-factor reasoning, and D47 covers simultaneous demand/supply shift ambiguity. No simultaneous-shift missing-unit flag remains for the target registry.\",\n        \"blocks\": [],\n        \"does_not_block\": [\n          \"Separate target-equivalent lesson proof review\",\n          \"Separate graph/table evidence closure\",\n          \"Separate Year 1 / CP-6 / Scale Gate closure review\",\n          \"Product-route adoption, diagnostics, mastery, PV, and student/product use gates\"\n        ],\n        \"proof_required_to_close\": \"Registry validator, unit catalog validator, review-throughput packet, and generated metadata checks pass; later product proof remains separate.\"\n      },\n      \"review_evidence\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-SIMSHIFT-PROTECTED-REFERENCE-IMPLEMENTATION-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-15\",\n        \"design_decision_packet\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-SIMSHIFT-MISSING-UNIT-DESIGN-1-review-packet.md\",\n        \"implementation_review_packet\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-SIMSHIFT-PROTECTED-REFERENCE-IMPLEMENTATION-1-review-packet.md\",\n        \"quality_log\": \"reports/reference-planning/B1-SIMSHIFT-PROTECTED-REFERENCE-IMPLEMENTATION-1-quality-log.md\",\n        \"source_pr\": \"https://github.com/meijer1973/4veco-platform/pull/68\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"D47 was minted through governed unit CLI as the simultaneous demand/supply shift operation and mapped into 1.3.3 so the target registry no longer carries the simultaneous-shift missing-unit flag.\",\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Protected-reference and authored target-registry implementation only; does not close Year 1, CP-6, target-equivalent lesson proof, generated lesson output, Scale Gate authority, diagnostics, mastery, PV, product-route adoption, or student-facing product use.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"difficulty\": \"MIXED\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"In the market for notebooks, students use demand and supply functions, a non-equilibrium price, and two separate one-shift scenarios to combine supply shifts, demand shifts, equilibrium calculation, graphing, surplus/shortage reasoning, and new-equilibrium comparison from 1.3.1-1.3.3. The target introduces no new economic theory and keeps simultaneous-shift reasoning out of the replacement path.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"For Qv = -2P + 120 and Qa = 3P - 30, calculate the original equilibrium price and quantity and mark the point in a graph.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"At P = EUR 35, determine whether there is a surplus or shortage and calculate its size.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A new printing machine increases supply to Qa = 3P - 15. Calculate the new equilibrium after only the supply increase and explain the direction of the supply shift.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"In a separate scenario, a school campaign raises demand to Qv = -2P + 140 while supply remains Qa = 3P - 30. Calculate the new equilibrium after only the demand increase and explain the direction of the demand shift.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Compare both one-shift scenarios with the original equilibrium. What happens to price and quantity after the supply increase, and what happens after the demand increase?\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n          \"Algebraic equations with enough working to check the equilibrium.\",\n          \"Graph with correctly labelled demand, supply, and equilibrium points.\",\n          \"Surplus/shortage answer that identifies which quantity is larger.\",\n          \"Written comparison of the two separate one-shift scenarios without importing simultaneous-shift reasoning.\"\n        ],\n        \"short_answer_model\": [\n          \"a. -2P + 120 = 3P - 30, so 150 = 5P, P = 30, Q = 60.\",\n          \"b. At P = 35, Qv = 50 and Qa = 75; there is an aanbodoverschot of 25 notebooks.\",\n          \"c. -2P + 120 = 3P - 15, so 135 = 5P, P = 27, Q = 66. Supply shifts right because producers supply more at each price.\",\n          \"d. -2P + 140 = 3P - 30, so 170 = 5P, P = 34, Q = 72. Demand shifts right because buyers want more at each price.\",\n          \"e. The supply increase lowers price from 30 to 27 and raises quantity from 60 to 66. The demand increase raises price from 30 to 34 and raises quantity from 60 to 72.\"\n        ],\n        \"evidence_path\": [\n          \"reports/reference-planning/Y1-PLACEHOLDER-TARGET-REVIEW-1-review-packet.md\",\n          \"reports/reference-planning/Y1-PLACEHOLDER-TARGET-REVIEW-1-quality-log.md\",\n          \"reports/reference-planning/CP.6c-mtu-backfill-classification.md\"\n        ],\n        \"no_new_theory_rationale\": \"The task consolidates supply shifts, demand shifts, equilibrium calculation, graphing, surplus/shortage, and new-equilibrium comparison from 1.3.1-1.3.3. It deliberately avoids costs, revenue, marginal analysis, welfare, taxes, subsidies, price controls, and simultaneous-shift ambiguity.\",\n        \"review_disposition\": \"accepted_for_governed_registry_replacement\"\n      },\n      \"mixed_target_profile\": {\n        \"integrates_paragraphs\": [\n          \"1.3.1\",\n          \"1.3.2\",\n          \"1.3.3\"\n        ],\n        \"source_selection_required\": true,\n        \"answer_construction_required\": true,\n        \"table_or_graph_interpretation_required\": true,\n        \"no_new_theory\": true\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Consolidate supply shifts, demand shifts, equilibrium calculation, surplus/shortage reasoning, and new-equilibrium comparison.\",\n        \"Select and apply the relevant demand or supply function for each one-shift scenario.\",\n        \"Compare price and quantity outcomes after separate shifts without using simultaneous-shift reasoning.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A02\",\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A42\",\n        \"A49\",\n        \"A51\",\n        \"D13\",\n        \"D32\",\n        \"D33\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A02\",\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A42\",\n        \"A49\",\n        \"A51\",\n        \"D13\",\n        \"D32\",\n        \"D33\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Reviewed integration target accepted from PR #42. It introduces no new theory. The candidate intentionally keeps simultaneous demand+supply shift reasoning out of the replacement path; that remains a separate bounded missing-unit design lane.\",\n      \"id\": \"1.3.4\",\n      \"module\": 1,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 4,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Gemengde opgaven: aanbod en marktevenwicht\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"gemengde_opgaven\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": false,\n      \"record_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §1.3.4\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"reviewed_final\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": null,\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": false,\n        \"review_completed_by\": \"B1-PLACEHOLDER-REGISTRY-REPLACEMENT-1\",\n        \"review_completed_on\": \"2026-06-12\"\n      },\n      \"review_evidence\": {\n        \"sprint_id\": \"B1-PLACEHOLDER-REGISTRY-REPLACEMENT-1\",\n        \"review_date\": \"2026-06-12\",\n        \"candidate_packet\": \"reports/reference-planning/Y1-PLACEHOLDER-TARGET-REVIEW-1-review-packet.md\",\n        \"quality_log\": \"reports/reference-planning/Y1-PLACEHOLDER-TARGET-REVIEW-1-quality-log.md\",\n        \"source_pr\": \"https://github.com/meijer1973/4veco-platform/pull/42\",\n        \"decision_summary\": \"Accepted revised PR #42 one-shift-at-a-time candidate for governed authored-registry replacement. Simultaneous-shift reasoning remains out of scope and stays in a separate missing-unit lane.\",\n        \"authority_boundary\": \"Reviewed source-registry replacement only; does not close Year 1, CP-6, target-equivalent proof, 1.3.3 simultaneous-shift missing-unit design, lesson output, Scale Gate authority, or student-facing product use.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.1.1\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 1,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Kostenstructuren\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Bakkerij De Korenaar produceert in één maand tussen 500 en 1.000 broden. Zolang de productiecapaciteit gelijk blijft, gebruikt de bakkerij dezelfde ruimte en ovens. Huur en verzekering kosten samen € 440 per maand. De netaansluiting en het energieabonnement kosten € 60 per maand. Meel en verpakking kosten samen € 0,70 per brood. Het elektriciteitsverbruik van de ovens kost binnen deze grenzen € 0,10 per brood. Q is het aantal broden per maand.\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"classificatietabel\",\n            \"type\": \"table\",\n            \"columns\": [\n              \"kostencomponent\",\n              \"soort kosten\",\n              \"reden\"\n            ],\n            \"rows\": [\n              [\n                \"huur en verzekering\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"netaansluiting en energieabonnement\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"meel en verpakking\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"elektriciteitsverbruik ovens\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\"\n              ]\n            ],\n            \"content\": \"Vul de lege cellen in.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Vul de classificatietabel in: classificeer elk onderdeel als constante of variabele kosten en noteer per onderdeel kort hoe het totale bedrag reageert op een verandering van Q.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Stel de functies voor TCK, TVK en TK op.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken bij Q = 500 de GCK, GVK en GTK. Noteer de volledige eenheden.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken bij Q = 1.000 de GCK, GVK en GTK. Noteer de volledige eenheden.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Vergelijk eerst TCK, TVK en TK bij Q = 500 en Q = 1.000. Leg daarna met de uitkomsten uit waarom GCK halveert, GVK gelijk blijft en GTK niet halveert als Q verdubbelt. Begrens je uitleg tot één maand, Q = 500–1.000 en gelijkblijvende productiecapaciteit.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt kostencomponenten als constant of variabel classificeren op basis van hun gedrag binnen een genoemde periode en bij gelijkblijvende productiecapaciteit.\",\n        \"Je kunt uit een context de functies voor TCK, TVK en TK opstellen.\",\n        \"Je kunt gemiddelde kosten berekenen en met de juiste eenheid interpreteren.\",\n        \"Je kunt verklaren hoe totale en gemiddelde kosten veranderen als Q verandert, binnen de aannames van het model.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A08\",\n        \"A14\",\n        \"A50\",\n        \"A86\",\n        \"D38\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A04: substitution and endpoint-unit checking only; formal economic cost classification and euro-per-period versus euro-per-product dimensions are new here.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A08\",\n        \"A14\",\n        \"A50\",\n        \"A86\",\n        \"D38\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Behaviour-based constant/variable classification within an explicit period and range.\",\n        \"D38 is semantically relevant, but its D02 prerequisite introduces deferred profit/MO=MK content and is not assumed here.\",\n        \"Conditional explanation of constant GVK under proportional variable cost.\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"D1.16\",\n        \"D1.17\",\n        \"A2.10\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Kandidaat voor circa 11 minuten: één classificatietabel, drie functies, twee compacte gemiddelde-kostenrijen en één begrensde vergelijking van totale en gemiddelde kosten.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"classificatietabel met reden\",\n        \"drie functies\",\n        \"twee gemiddelde-kostenrijen met eenheid\",\n        \"totale-kostenvergelijking en drie begrensde gemiddelde-kostenverklaringen\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"a\": \"Huur/verzekering en aansluiting/abonnement zijn constant: hun totale maandbedrag verandert niet met Q. Meel/verpakking en ovenverbruik zijn variabel: hun totale bedrag neemt per brood toe.\",\n        \"b\": \"TCK = 500; TVK = 0,80Q; TK = 500 + 0,80Q (bedragen in euro per maand).\",\n        \"c\": \"Bij Q=500: GCK=500/500=€1,00 per brood; GVK=0,80×500/500=€0,80 per brood; GTK=500/500+0,80=€1,80 per brood.\",\n        \"d\": \"Bij Q=1.000: GCK=€0,50 per brood; GVK=€0,80 per brood; GTK=€1,30 per brood.\",\n        \"e\": \"Bij Q=500 zijn TCK=€500, TVK=€400 en TK=€900 per maand; bij Q=1.000 zijn TCK=€500, TVK=€800 en TK=€1.300 per maand. TCK blijft gelijk, TVK verdubbelt en TK stijgt maar verdubbelt niet. GCK halveert van €1,00 naar €0,50 per brood doordat dezelfde TCK over tweemaal zoveel broden worden verdeeld. GVK blijft €0,80 doordat de variabele kosten per brood gelijk blijven. GTK daalt van €1,80 naar €1,30 en halveert niet, omdat alleen GCK halveert. Dit geldt binnen de genoemde maand, Q-grenzen en gelijkblijvende productiecapaciteit.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.1.1\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"1.3.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.1.2\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 2,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Opbrengsten, winst en break-even\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"LIGHT\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Bakkerij De Korenaar heeft TK = 500 + 0,80Q en verkoopt ieder brood voor een vaste prijs van € 1,50. Q is het aantal broden per maand; alle bedragen zijn in euro per maand tenzij anders vermeld.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Stel de functie voor TO op. Leg voor Q>0 uit waarom GO in deze context gelijk is aan de prijs.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken de winst bij Q=500 en bij Q=1.000.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Los algebraïsch TO=TK op. Noteer zowel de continue break-even-afzet als het eerste gehele aantal broden waarbij geen verlies ontstaat.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Teken TK en TO in één assenstelsel. Benoem beide assen met grootheid en eenheid, markeer het break-evenpunt en de winst- en verlieszone en geef bij Q=1.000 de winst van €200 weer als verticale afstand. Kleur geen oppervlakte als winst.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt TO, GO en winst berekenen en GO bij een vaste prijs verklaren.\",\n        \"Je kunt de break-even-afzet algebraïsch bepalen en een continue uitkomst onderscheiden van een geheel productaantal.\",\n        \"Je kunt TK en TO in één grafiek weergeven en winst- en verlieszones herkennen.\",\n        \"Je kunt winst bij een gegeven Q als verticale afstand tussen TO en TK weergeven.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A07\",\n        \"A08\",\n        \"A21\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A08 and the §2.1.1 cost functions are retrieval-required.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A07\",\n        \"A21\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Linear break-even-afzet from TK and TO without a quadratic method.\",\n        \"Draw and interpret linear TK and TO in one graph, including break-even and vertical profit distance.\",\n        \"Explain GO=P under a constant unit price.\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.1\",\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.14\",\n        \"D1.14\",\n        \"D1.19\",\n        \"D1.20\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Kandidaat voor circa 12 minuten: twee korte berekeningen, één algebraïsche oplossing en vijf minuten voor de complete grafiek.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"functie en contextverklaring\",\n        \"twee winstberekeningen\",\n        \"algebra met afrondingsduiding\",\n        \"gelabelde grafiek met verticale winstafstand\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"a\": \"TO=1,50Q. Omdat TO=P×Q en P constant is, geldt voor Q>0: GO=(P×Q)/Q=P=€1,50 per brood.\",\n        \"b\": \"Q=500: winst=€750−€900=−€150. Q=1.000: winst=€1.500−€1.300=€200.\",\n        \"c\": \"1,50Q=500+0,80Q, dus 0,70Q=500 en Q=714,2857…. In het continue model is de break-even-afzet Q=714,29; het eerste gehele aantal broden zonder verlies is 715 (winst €0,50).\",\n        \"d\": \"Horizontale as: hoeveelheid Q (broden per maand). Verticale as: TK en TO (€ per maand). TK start bij €500 en heeft helling 0,80; TO start bij €0 en heeft helling 1,50. Snijpunt: (714,29; 1.071,43). Links ligt de verlieszone en rechts de winstzone. Bij Q=1.000 is de verticale afstand €1.500−€1.300=€200.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.1.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"1.3.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.1.3\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 3,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Marginale kosten en marginale opbrengsten\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Onderneming Linea heeft TK = 200 + 3Q en TO = 8Q. Onderneming Curva heeft TK = 100 + Q² en verkoopt ieder product voor € 30, zodat TO = 30Q. Bedragen zijn in euro. Bereken marginale waarden per extra product over ieder tabelinterval: MK = ΔTK/ΔQ en MO = ΔTO/ΔQ. Gebruik geen afgeleiden.\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"tabel-linea\",\n            \"type\": \"table\",\n            \"columns\": [\n              \"Q\",\n              \"TK\",\n              \"TO\",\n              \"winst\",\n              \"MK\",\n              \"MO\"\n            ],\n            \"rows\": [\n              [\n                \"0\",\n                \"€200\",\n                \"€0\",\n                \"\",\n                \"—\",\n                \"—\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"10\",\n                \"€230\",\n                \"€80\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"20\",\n                \"€260\",\n                \"€160\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"30\",\n                \"€290\",\n                \"€240\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\"\n              ]\n            ],\n            \"content\": \"Vul de lege cellen in. Noteer een intervalwaarde voor MK en MO steeds bij het rechter eindpunt.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"tabel-curva\",\n            \"type\": \"table\",\n            \"columns\": [\n              \"Q\",\n              \"TK\",\n              \"TO\",\n              \"winst\",\n              \"MK\",\n              \"MO\"\n            ],\n            \"rows\": [\n              [\n                \"0\",\n                \"€100\",\n                \"€0\",\n                \"−€100\",\n                \"—\",\n                \"—\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"5\",\n                \"€125\",\n                \"€150\",\n                \"€25\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"10\",\n                \"€200\",\n                \"€300\",\n                \"€100\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"15\",\n                \"€325\",\n                \"€450\",\n                \"€125\",\n                \"\",\n                \"\"\n              ]\n            ],\n            \"content\": \"Vul de lege cellen in. Noteer een intervalwaarde voor MK en MO steeds bij het rechter eindpunt.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Vul in de tabel van Linea de winstkolom in.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken voor Linea MK en MO over het eerste interval. Laat teller en noemer zien en vul dezelfde intervalwaarden in bij de rechter eindpunten Q=10, Q=20 en Q=30.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Leg uit waarom MO bij Linea constant is.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken voor Curva MK over de drie intervallen en MO over het eerste interval. Vul de berekende MK- en MO-waarden in bij de juiste rechter eindpunten Q=5, Q=10 en Q=15.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Vergelijk het MK-patroon van Linea en Curva en leg uit wat MK en MO per extra product binnen een tabelinterval betekenen. Trek geen conclusie over de winstmaximaliserende afzet.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt MK als ΔTK/ΔQ en MO als ΔTO/ΔQ over een tabelinterval berekenen.\",\n        \"Je kunt gegeven totale kosten en totale opbrengsten gebruiken om een winstkolom in te vullen.\",\n        \"Je kunt constante en stijgende marginale patronen vergelijken zonder afgeleiden.\",\n        \"Je kunt uitleggen wat MK en MO per extra product binnen het genoemde tabelinterval betekenen.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A07\",\n        \"A08\",\n        \"A21\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A07, A08 and A21 totals are retrieval-required; the interval method is taught as new formal learning.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"Table-interval MK=ΔTK/ΔQ\",\n        \"Table-interval MO=ΔTO/ΔQ\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"A dedicated pre-calculus table-interval unit for MK and MO.\",\n        \"Pattern comparison for linear versus quadratic total costs.\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.1\",\n        \"D1.14\",\n        \"D1.17\",\n        \"D1.19\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Kandidaat voor circa 12 minuten met twee echte tabellen. De tweede tabel is vooraf grotendeels ingevuld; de intervalnoemer en rechter eindpunten blijven zichtbaar.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"winstkolom\",\n        \"intervalbreuken met ΔQ op de rechter eindpunten\",\n        \"korte patroonverklaring\",\n        \"begrensde betekenis\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"a\": \"Linea bij Q=0/10/20/30: winst=−€200/−€150/−€100/−€50.\",\n        \"b\": \"Eerste interval: MK=(230−200)/(10−0)=€3 per product en MO=(80−0)/(10−0)=€8 per product. Zet MK=€3 en MO=€8 bij de rechter eindpunten Q=10, Q=20 en Q=30.\",\n        \"c\": \"Linea heeft constant MK=€3 en MO=€8 per product. Bij een vaste prijs levert ieder extra verkocht product dezelfde extra opbrengst op.\",\n        \"d\": \"Curva: MK=(125−100)/(5−0)=€5 bij Q=5, MK=€15 bij Q=10 en MK=€25 bij Q=15. MO=(150−0)/(5−0)=€30 en staat als constante intervalwaarde bij Q=5, Q=10 en Q=15.\",\n        \"e\": \"Linea heeft constant MK; Curva heeft stijgend MK. MK is de gemiddelde extra totale kosten per extra product binnen het interval; MO is de gemiddelde extra totale opbrengst per extra product binnen het interval.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.1.3\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"1.4.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.1.4\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 4,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Gemengde opgaven: kosten en opbrengsten\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MIXED\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"SmoothBox verkoopt lunchboxen op een schoolfestival. Gebruik bron A voor normale productie tot en met de capaciteit en bron B voor extra spoedproductie. De opgave combineert alleen eerder onderwezen kosten-, opbrengst-, break-even- en marginale handelingen.\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"bron-a\",\n            \"content\": \"Per festivaldag: TCK €1.200; variabele kosten €2 per lunchbox; verkoopprijs €5 per lunchbox; normale capaciteit 700 lunchboxen. Er worden 4.000 bezoekers verwacht.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"bron-b\",\n            \"type\": \"table\",\n            \"columns\": [\n              \"Q\",\n              \"TK\",\n              \"TO\"\n            ],\n            \"rows\": [\n              [\n                \"700\",\n                \"€2.600\",\n                \"€3.500\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"800\",\n                \"€2.900\",\n                \"€4.000\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"900\",\n                \"€3.250\",\n                \"€4.500\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"1.000\",\n                \"€3.650\",\n                \"€5.000\"\n              ]\n            ],\n            \"content\": \"Tabel met totale kosten en totale opbrengst tijdens spoedproductie.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"basisgrafiek\",\n            \"content\": \"Een assenstelsel met horizontaal hoeveelheid Q (lunchboxen per dag) van 0 tot 1.000 en verticaal TK en TO (€ per dag) van €0 tot €5.000. De lijnen TK en TO en de punten uit bron A en B zijn al getekend en gelabeld; break-even en het gevraagde winstinterval zijn nog niet gemarkeerd.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"1\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Selecteer uit bron A TCK, de variabele kosten per lunchbox en de prijs. Classificeer TCK als constant en de kosten per lunchbox als variabel. Noem ook het gegeven dat niet nodig is.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"2\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Stel voor 0 ≤ Q ≤ 700 de functies voor TK en TO op en bereken de break-even-afzet.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"3\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken bij Q=700 de winst en GTK, beide met eenheid.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"4\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken met bron B voor elk spoedinterval MK en MO per lunchbox.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"5\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Markeer in de basisgrafiek het break-evenpunt en het bereik waarin de positieve verticale afstand tussen TO en TK het snelst groeit. Leg met MK en MO uit waarom en vergelijk normale productie met alle spoedintervallen.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"6\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Een leerling zegt: ‘Door de vaste verkoopprijs leveren alle extra groepen van 100 lunchboxen evenveel extra winst op.’ Beoordeel deze uitspraak met de uitkomsten van vraag 4.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"mixed_target_profile\": {\n        \"integrates_paragraphs\": [\n          \"2.1.1\",\n          \"2.1.2\",\n          \"2.1.3\"\n        ],\n        \"source_selection_required\": true,\n        \"answer_construction_required\": true,\n        \"table_or_graph_interpretation_required\": true,\n        \"no_new_theory\": true\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt relevante gegevens selecteren en kosten-, opbrengst- en break-evenhandelingen combineren.\",\n        \"Je kunt gemiddelde en marginale uitkomsten berekenen en met eenheid vergelijken.\",\n        \"Je kunt tabel- en grafiekrepresentaties verbinden om een winstontwikkeling te verklaren.\",\n        \"Je kunt een economische claim met berekeningen beoordelen zonder verder te gaan dan de gegevens.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A07\",\n        \"A08\",\n        \"A14\",\n        \"A21\",\n        \"A50\",\n        \"A86\",\n        \"D38\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"All listed skills are retrieval from §§2.1.1–2.1.3; no optimization rule is assumed.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.1\",\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.14\",\n        \"D1.14\",\n        \"D1.16\",\n        \"D1.17\",\n        \"D1.19\",\n        \"D1.20\",\n        \"D1.21\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Gemengde kandidaat voor circa 20 minuten met vijf vragen van twee punten en één vierpunts-intervaltabel. Iedere vraag vraagt één afgebakende bewijsstap.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"bronselectie\",\n        \"functies en break-even-afzet\",\n        \"één rekenset\",\n        \"drie intervalberekeningen\",\n        \"grafiekmarkering met volledige vergelijking\",\n        \"claimbeoordeling\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"1\": \"TCK=€1.200 per dag en is constant; de variabele kosten zijn €2 per lunchbox; P=€5 per lunchbox. De 4.000 bezoekers zijn niet nodig voor de gevraagde kost- en opbrengstfuncties.\",\n        \"2\": \"Voor 0≤Q≤700: TK=1.200+2Q en TO=5Q. Break-even: 5Q=1.200+2Q, dus Q=400 lunchboxen.\",\n        \"3\": \"Bij Q=700 is de winst €3.500−€2.600=€900 per dag en GTK=€2.600/700≈€3,71 per lunchbox.\",\n        \"4\": \"Spoedintervallen Q=700–800/800–900/900–1.000: MK=€3,00/€3,50/€4,00 per lunchbox; MO is steeds €5,00 per lunchbox.\",\n        \"5\": \"Break-even ligt bij Q=400. Bij normale productie van Q=400–700 geldt MO−MK=€5−€2=€3 per lunchbox. In de spoedintervallen is MO−MK achtereenvolgens €2,00, €1,50 en €1,00. De positieve verticale winstafstand groeit dus het snelst van Q=400 tot Q=700.\",\n        \"6\": \"Onjuist. Uit vraag 4 volgt dat MO−MK in de spoedintervallen daalt van €2,00 via €1,50 naar €1,00 per lunchbox. De extra winst per groep van 100 is dus €200, €150 en €100.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.1.4\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"gemengde_opgaven\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": false,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": null,\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.2.1\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 1,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Prijselasticiteit\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Bioscoop Nova verhoogt de ticketprijs van €10 naar €12; de gevraagde hoeveelheid daalt van 500 naar 420 tickets per week. Bij StreamNow is voor een andere prijsverhoging Ev=−2 gemeten. Gebruik voor Nova de oude waarde als noemer en Ev=%ΔQv/%ΔP.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken voor Bioscoop Nova de procentuele prijsverandering, de procentuele verandering van de gevraagde hoeveelheid en Ev.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Classificeer de vraag naar bioscoopkaartjes met |Ev| en leg in gewone taal uit wat Ev=−0,8 betekent.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Classificeer de vraag naar StreamNow met Ev=−2 en vergelijk de prijsgevoeligheid met die van Bioscoop Nova.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Geef één plausibele contextverklaring voor het verschil in prijsgevoeligheid. Baseer je verklaring niet op een andere elasticiteitssoort.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt procentuele prijs- en hoeveelheidsveranderingen met de oude waarde als noemer berekenen.\",\n        \"Je kunt Ev inclusief het teken berekenen.\",\n        \"Je kunt vraag met |Ev| classificeren als prijselastisch of prijsinelastisch.\",\n        \"Je kunt verschillen in prijsgevoeligheid met contextgegevens verklaren zonder verder te gaan dan de gegevens.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A15\",\n        \"A38\",\n        \"D06\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A38 percentage change and Book 1 demand/substitute reasoning are explicit retrieval, not silent mastery.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A15\",\n        \"D06\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"D06 carries a stale D1.3 exam-code mapping; this record follows the current D1.5–D1.6 syllabus operations.\",\n        \"A15 and A38 carry broad A2.5 metadata, but this target contains no percentage-point operation and does not claim A2.5 coverage.\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.4\",\n        \"D1.5\",\n        \"D1.6\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Kandidaat voor circa 9 minuten: één volledige Ev-berekening, twee classificaties en één contextverklaring die niet verder gaat dan de gegevens.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"procentberekening en Ev inclusief teken\",\n        \"classificatie in gewone taal\",\n        \"vergelijking\",\n        \"plausibele verklaring\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"a\": \"%ΔP=(12−10)/10×100%=+20%; %ΔQv=(420−500)/500×100%=−16%; Ev=−16%/+20%=−0,8.\",\n        \"b\": \"|−0,8|<1: prijsinelastische vraag. De gevraagde hoeveelheid daalt procentueel minder dan de prijs stijgt.\",\n        \"c\": \"|−2|>1: prijselastische vraag. StreamNow reageert in deze waarneming procentueel sterker op de prijsverandering dan Bioscoop Nova.\",\n        \"d\": \"Bijvoorbeeld: streamingabonnementen hebben meer direct vergelijkbare alternatieven of zijn makkelijker uit te stellen. Dit is plausibel, niet door de cijfers bewezen.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.2.1\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"2.1.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.2.2\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 2,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Elasticiteit en omzet\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Gebruik TO=P×Q. Bij Bioscoop Nova stijgt P van €10 naar €12 en daalt Q van 500 naar 420 per week; Ev=−0,8. Bij StreamNow stijgt P van €20 naar €22 en daalt Q van 1.000 naar 800 per maand; Ev=−2,0. Alle gegevens die je nodig hebt staan hier.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken voor Bioscoop Nova TO vóór en na de prijsverhoging.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken de procentuele verandering van TO bij Bioscoop Nova en verbind die uitkomst met Ev=−0,8.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken voor StreamNow TO vóór en na de prijsverhoging.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken de procentuele verandering van TO bij StreamNow en verbind die uitkomst met Ev=−2.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Formuleer de lokale omzetregel voor een kleine prijsstijging bij prijsinelastische en bij prijselastische vraag. Leg uit waarom je bij een grote, eindige verandering altijd TO vóór en na berekent.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"f\",\n            \"points\": 1,\n            \"prompt\": \"Leg uit waarom uit deze omzetgegevens niet volgt dat de winst stijgt.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt totale opbrengst vóór en na een prijsverandering berekenen.\",\n        \"Je kunt de lokale omzetrichting bij een kleine prijsverandering en prijselastische of prijsinelastische vraag verklaren.\",\n        \"Je kunt een waargenomen omzeteffect met Ev onderbouwen.\",\n        \"Je kunt uitleggen waarom omzetgegevens zonder kostengegevens geen winstconclusie dragen.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A15\",\n        \"A85\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A15 and A85 are retrieval-required.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"Local small-change revenue direction with a direct finite-change check\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"The machine-unit library has no valid unit for the local Ev/TO rule. D25 concerns a different mathematical skill and must not be cited as coverage; finite changes require direct before/after revenue calculation.\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.4\",\n        \"D1.14\",\n        \"D1.15\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Kandidaat voor circa 11 minuten. Iedere vraag levert één korte berekening of afgebakende conclusie op.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"oude/nieuwe TO\",\n        \"procentuele TO-verandering met Ev\",\n        \"tweezijdige lokale regel\",\n        \"winstbeperking\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"a\": \"TO oud=10×500=€5.000 per week; TO nieuw=12×420=€5.040 per week.\",\n        \"b\": \"TO stijgt met €40/€5.000×100%=0,8%. Dat past bij |Ev|=0,8<1: de procentuele daling van Q is kleiner dan de procentuele prijsstijging.\",\n        \"c\": \"TO oud=20×1.000=€20.000 per maand; TO nieuw=22×800=€17.600 per maand.\",\n        \"d\": \"%ΔTO=(17.600−20.000)/20.000×100%=−12%. TO daalt dus met 12%. Dat past bij |Ev|=2>1: de procentuele daling van Q is groter dan de procentuele prijsstijging.\",\n        \"e\": \"Lokale regel: bij een kleine prijsstijging stijgt TO bij prijsinelastische vraag en daalt TO bij prijselastische vraag. Bij een grote, eindige verandering kan het product van beide procentuele factoren afwijken; daarom bereken je TO vóór en na rechtstreeks.\",\n        \"f\": \"Winst is TO−TK. Omdat kostengegevens ontbreken, bewijzen de omzetgegevens geen hogere winst.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.2.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"2.1.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.2.3\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 3,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Inkomenselasticiteit en kruislingse elasticiteit\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Gebruik steeds de oude waarde als noemer. Voor inkomenselasticiteit geldt Ei=%ΔQv/%ΔY. De drie gebruikte categorieën zijn: Ei<0 inferieur goed, 0<Ei<1 normaal goed en Ei>1 luxegoed; Ei=0 en Ei=1 zijn grenswaarden. Voor kruislingse elasticiteit geldt Ek=%ΔQv van goed X/%ΔP van een ander goed Z. Noem bij Ek altijd beide goederen.\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"inkomen\",\n            \"content\": \"Het gemiddelde inkomen stijgt 5%. De vraag naar maaltijdpakketten stijgt 8%; de vraag naar budgetnoedels daalt 3%.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"koffie\",\n            \"content\": \"De koffieprijs stijgt 10%. De vraag naar thee stijgt 4%; de vraag naar koffiefilters daalt 6%.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"functie\",\n            \"content\": \"Voor fitnessdienst X geldt Qx=100−2Px+0,5Pz+0,01Y. Qx is het aantal gevraagde abonnementen per maand, Px en Pz zijn maandprijzen in euro van fitnessdienst X en concurrerende sportdienst Z, en Y is het gemiddelde jaarinkomen in euro. Beginsituatie: Px=10, Pz=20 en Y=30.000.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken met bron inkomen Ei voor maaltijdpakketten en budgetnoedels.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Classificeer beide goederen met de drie categorieën uit de context.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken met bron koffie Ek voor de vraag naar thee ten opzichte van de koffieprijs en voor de vraag naar koffiefilters ten opzichte van de koffieprijs. Noem teller- en noemergoed en classificeer de relaties.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken Qx in de beginsituatie en nadat alleen Y stijgt naar 33.000. Bereken daarna %ΔQx, Ei en classificeer fitnessdienst X. Leg uit wat met Qx gebeurt en welke variabelen gelijk blijven.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Zet Y terug op 30.000. Bereken Qx nadat alleen Pz stijgt van 20 naar 24. Leg uit welke richting het verband tussen Pz en Qx heeft en welke variabelen gelijk blijven.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt Ei berekenen en een goed classificeren als inferieur goed, normaal goed of luxegoed.\",\n        \"Je kunt Ek berekenen, beide goederen noemen en substituten of complementen herkennen.\",\n        \"Je kunt een vraagfunctie met meerdere variabelen gebruiken terwijl de overige variabelen gelijk blijven.\",\n        \"Je kunt uit twee functie-uitkomsten afleiden hoe één veranderde variabele samenhangt met Q terwijl de andere variabelen gelijk blijven.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A15\",\n        \"A16\",\n        \"A17\",\n        \"D11\",\n        \"D12\",\n        \"D27\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A04 and D27 are retrieval-required; A15 from §2.2.1 supplies the percentage-change procedure; Ei and Ek are new formal learning.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A16\",\n        \"A17\",\n        \"D11\",\n        \"D12\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"A16 and D12 use stale Ekr/kruiselasticiteit wording. This candidate follows the authored authority: kruislingse elasticiteit with notation Ek; CvTE specifies the term but no abbreviation.\",\n        \"A17 depends on A15, which is explicitly retrieved from §2.2.1.\",\n        \"A15, A16 and A17 carry broad A2.5 metadata, but this target contains no percentage-point operation and does not claim A2.5 coverage.\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.4\",\n        \"D1.7\",\n        \"D1.8\",\n        \"D1.9\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Kandidaat voor circa 11 minuten. De bronpercentages leveren luxe en inferieur; de inkomensmutatie in de functie levert aanvullend de categorie normaal. De laatste functie-invulling vraagt richting en gelijkblijvende variabelen.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"Ei inclusief teken\",\n        \"driewegclassificatie\",\n        \"Ek met benoemde goederen\",\n        \"functie-invulling met Ei-normaalclassificatie en een functie-invulling met richting\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"a\": \"Maaltijdpakketten: Ei=+8%/+5%=+1,6. Budgetnoedels: Ei=−3%/+5%=−0,6.\",\n        \"b\": \"Maaltijdpakketten: luxegoed (Ei>1). Budgetnoedels: inferieur goed (Ei<0).\",\n        \"c\": \"Theevraag ten opzichte van koffieprijs: Ek=+4%/+10%=+0,4, dus thee en koffie zijn substituten. Vraag naar koffiefilters ten opzichte van koffieprijs: Ek=−6%/+10%=−0,6, dus koffiefilters en koffie zijn complementen.\",\n        \"d\": \"Qx oud=100−20+10+300=390 abonnementen per maand. Bij Y=33.000: Qx=420. %ΔQx=(420−390)/390×100%=7,69% en %ΔY=(33.000−30.000)/30.000×100%=10%. Ei=7,69%/10%=0,769, dus fitnessdienst X is hier een normaal goed. Qx stijgt met 30; Px en Pz blijven gelijk.\",\n        \"e\": \"Na reset geldt Qx=390. Bij Pz=24: Qx=100−20+12+300=392 abonnementen per maand. Een hogere prijs van sportdienst Z hangt in deze functie samen met een hogere vraag naar fitnessdienst X; Px en Y blijven gelijk.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.2.3\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"2.1.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.2.4\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 4,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Gemengde opgaven: elasticiteit\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MIXED\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"StreamPlus onderzoekt één recente prijsverhoging en mogelijke vervolgstappen. Gebruik bij vragen 1 tot en met 5 telkens alleen de aangegeven bron. Combineer bij vraag 6 minstens twee bronnen. De gemeten Ev in bron A is gegeven; de opgave combineert eerder onderwezen handelingen en introduceert geen nieuwe formule.\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"bron-a\",\n            \"type\": \"table\",\n            \"columns\": [\n              \"gegeven\",\n              \"oud\",\n              \"nieuw\"\n            ],\n            \"rows\": [\n              [\n                \"prijs Standaard\",\n                \"€10\",\n                \"€12\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"abonnees\",\n                \"50.000\",\n                \"43.000\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"appscore\",\n                \"4,6/5\",\n                \"4,6/5\"\n              ]\n            ],\n            \"content\": \"Tabel met oude en nieuwe prijs, aantal abonnees en appscore; de gemeten Ev is −0,7.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"bron-b\",\n            \"type\": \"table\",\n            \"columns\": [\n              \"verandering\",\n              \"Premium\",\n              \"Budget\"\n            ],\n            \"rows\": [\n              [\n                \"inkomen\",\n                \"+8%\",\n                \"+8%\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"gevraagde hoeveelheid\",\n                \"+15%\",\n                \"−4%\"\n              ]\n            ],\n            \"content\": \"Tabel met inkomens- en vraagverandering voor Premium en Budget.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"bron-c\",\n            \"type\": \"table\",\n            \"columns\": [\n              \"gegeven\",\n              \"oud\",\n              \"nieuw\"\n            ],\n            \"rows\": [\n              [\n                \"prijs concurrerend abonnement\",\n                \"€8\",\n                \"€9\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"vraag StreamPlus\",\n                \"basis\",\n                \"+5%\"\n              ]\n            ],\n            \"content\": \"Tabel voor de kruislingse elasticiteit van StreamPlus ten opzichte van het concurrerende abonnement.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"bron-d\",\n            \"content\": \"Regionale vraagfunctie Q=12.000−400P+0,1Y+300Pc. Q is het aantal gevraagde abonnementen per maand, P en Pc zijn maandprijzen in euro en Y is het gemiddelde jaarinkomen in euro. Beginsituatie: P=12, Pc=10 en Y=40.000.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"1\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Selecteer uit bron A de gegevens voor een controleberekening van Ev en noem het gegeven dat daarvoor niet nodig is.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"2\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Classificeer met de gegeven Ev=−0,7 de vraag en leg in gewone taal uit hoe sterk Q procentueel reageert op een prijsverandering.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"3\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken met bron A TO vóór en na de prijsverhoging.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"4\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken en classificeer met bron B Ei voor Premium en Budget en met bron C Ek van de vraag naar StreamPlus ten opzichte van de prijs van het concurrerende abonnement. Noem bij Ek beide diensten.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"5\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken met bron D Q in de beginsituatie en nadat alleen Y stijgt naar €42.000 per jaar. Noem welke variabelen gelijk blijven.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"6\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Geef op basis van minstens twee bronnen een voorzichtig omzetadvies. Noem één conclusie die de bronnen ondersteunen en precies twee conclusies die zij niet bewijzen.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"mixed_target_profile\": {\n        \"integrates_paragraphs\": [\n          \"2.2.1\",\n          \"2.2.2\",\n          \"2.2.3\"\n        ],\n        \"source_selection_required\": true,\n        \"answer_construction_required\": true,\n        \"table_or_graph_interpretation_required\": true,\n        \"no_new_theory\": true\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt relevante tabelgegevens selecteren, Ev classificeren en uitleggen, en Ei en Ek berekenen en classificeren.\",\n        \"Je kunt totale opbrengst vóór en na een prijsverandering vergelijken.\",\n        \"Je kunt een vraagfunctie gebruiken als één variabele verandert en de andere variabelen gelijk blijven.\",\n        \"Je kunt met meerdere bronnen een omzetadvies formuleren zonder verder te gaan dan de gegevens.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A15\",\n        \"A16\",\n        \"A17\",\n        \"A38\",\n        \"A85\",\n        \"D06\",\n        \"D11\",\n        \"D12\",\n        \"D27\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"All listed skills are retrieval from §§2.2.1–2.2.3.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"A16 and D12 use stale Ekr/kruiselasticiteit wording. This candidate follows the authored authority: kruislingse elasticiteit with notation Ek.\",\n        \"The local Ev/TO rule has no valid machine-unit coverage; D25 is not cited.\",\n        \"A15, A16, A17 and A38 carry broad A2.5 metadata, but this target contains no percentage-point operation and does not claim A2.5 coverage.\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.4\",\n        \"D1.5\",\n        \"D1.6\",\n        \"D1.7\",\n        \"D1.8\",\n        \"D1.9\",\n        \"D1.14\",\n        \"D1.15\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Gemengde kandidaat voor circa 20 minuten met vijf vragen van twee punten, één gecombineerde elasticiteitsvraag van vier punten en vier compacte bronnen.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"bronselectie\",\n        \"Ev-uitleg in gewone taal\",\n        \"omzetvergelijking\",\n        \"gecombineerde Ei- en Ek-berekening\",\n        \"vraagfunctie-invulling\",\n        \"twee-bronnenadvies met beperkingen\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"1\": \"Nodig: P €10→€12 en Q 50.000→43.000. De appscore 4,6/5 is niet nodig voor Ev.\",\n        \"2\": \"|Ev|=0,7<1, dus de vraag is prijsinelastisch. Bij een prijsverandering van 1% verandert Q in deze waarneming in tegengestelde richting met ongeveer 0,7%.\",\n        \"3\": \"TO oud=€10×50.000=€500.000; TO nieuw=€12×43.000=€516.000.\",\n        \"4\": \"Premium: Ei=15%/8%=1,875, luxegoed. Budget: Ei=−4%/8%=−0,5, inferieur goed. Voor StreamPlus geldt %ΔP concurrent=(9−8)/8×100%=12,5% en Ek=5%/12,5%=0,4. Teller: vraag naar StreamPlus; noemer: prijs van de concurrerende dienst. Positief, dus de diensten zijn substituten.\",\n        \"5\": \"Q oud=12.000−4.800+4.000+3.000=14.200 abonnementen per maand. Bij Y=€42.000 per jaar is Q=14.400 abonnementen per maand; P en Pc blijven gelijk.\",\n        \"6\": \"Voorzichtig: de onderzochte verhoging verhoogde TO met €16.000; bron A ondersteunt die conclusie en bron C laat substitutie met de concurrent zien. Niet bewezen zijn precies: dezelfde reactie bij een volgende prijsverhoging en een hogere winst, omdat kostengegevens ontbreken.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.2.4\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"gemengde_opgaven\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": false,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": null,\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 1,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Consumentensurplus\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Voor concertkaartjes geldt de vraagfunctie P=50−0,5Q. P is de prijs in euro per kaartje en Q het aantal gevraagde kaartjes. De gegeven marktprijs is €20. Er zijn voldoende kaartjes beschikbaar; alle bij deze prijs gevraagde kaartjes worden verkocht aan de vragers met de hoogste betalingsbereidheid. Er is in deze opgave geen aanbodfunctie.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken de gevraagde hoeveelheid bij P=€20. Noem dit niet de evenwichtshoeveelheid.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Teken de vraaglijn en de horizontale prijslijn in één assenstelsel. Benoem de horizontale as als Q (kaartjes) en de verticale as als P (€ per kaartje). Noteer de snijpunten van de vraaglijn en het snijpunt met de prijslijn.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Arceer en benoem in je grafiek het consumentensurplus.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken het consumentensurplus als oppervlakte van een driehoek en noteer de eenheid.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Leg uit wat het berekende consumentensurplus voor de kopers als groep betekent.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt de gevraagde hoeveelheid bij een gegeven prijs uit een vraagfunctie berekenen.\",\n        \"Je kunt consumentensurplus in een vraag-prijsgrafiek herkennen en arceren.\",\n        \"Je kunt consumentensurplus als driehoeksoppervlakte berekenen.\",\n        \"Je kunt consumentensurplus als het gezamenlijke voordeel van alle kopers van de verkochte kaartjes interpreteren.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A03\",\n        \"A05\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A40\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A03, A05 and A10 are explicit retrieval; A40 and the demand-only surplus operation are new formal learning.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A40\",\n        \"Demand-only consumer-surplus calculation at an exogenous price\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"A dedicated unit for demand-only consumer surplus at an exogenous price without a market-equilibrium prerequisite.\",\n        \"Interpret aggregate willingness-to-pay minus paid price over realized transactions.\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.16\",\n        \"A2.17\",\n        \"D3.1\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Kandidaat voor circa 10 minuten. Zonder aanbodfunctie wordt Q=60 niet als evenwicht benoemd; de context legt verkoop en toewijzing van alle 60 gevraagde kaartjes expliciet vast.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"functie-oplossing\",\n        \"gelabelde grafiek\",\n        \"correct gebied\",\n        \"driehoeksberekening\",\n        \"koperinterpretatie\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"a\": \"20=50−0,5Q, dus de gevraagde hoeveelheid (Qd) is 60 kaartjes; dit is geen berekend marktevenwicht.\",\n        \"b\": \"Horizontale as: Q (kaartjes). Verticale as: P (€ per kaartje). Vraaglijn door (Q=0,P=50) en (Q=100,P=0); prijslijn P=20; snijpunt bij Q=60.\",\n        \"c\": \"Driehoek boven P=20 en onder de vraaglijn, van Q=0 tot Q=60, gelabeld CS.\",\n        \"d\": \"CS=½×60×(50−20)=€900.\",\n        \"e\": \"€900 is de som van het verschil tussen betalingsbereidheid en werkelijk betaalde prijs over de 60 verkochte kaartjes.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.3.1\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"2.2.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 2,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Producentensurplus en totaal surplus\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Voor concertkaartjes geldt vraag P=50−0,5Q en aanbod P=5+0,25Q. P is in euro per kaartje en Q in kaartjes. Binnen dit marktmodel stelt de inverse aanbodlijn voor 0≤Q≤100 de marginale kosten van iedere opeenvolgende eenheid voor, ook als die eenheid in het evenwicht niet wordt verkocht.\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"basisgrafiek\",\n            \"content\": \"Een assenstelsel met horizontaal Q (kaartjes) van 0 tot 100 en verticaal P (€ per kaartje) van €0 tot €50. De lijnen vraag en aanbod zijn volledig getekend en gelabeld; evenwicht, CS en PS zijn nog niet gemarkeerd.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"marginale-vergelijking\",\n            \"type\": \"table\",\n            \"columns\": [\n              \"Q\",\n              \"betalingsbereidheid volgens vraaglijn\",\n              \"marginale kosten volgens aanbodlijn\"\n            ],\n            \"rows\": [\n              [\n                \"50\",\n                \"€25\",\n                \"€17,50\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"70\",\n                \"€15\",\n                \"€22,50\"\n              ]\n            ],\n            \"content\": \"De waarden zijn met de gegeven vraag- en aanbodfunctie berekend.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken de evenwichtsprijs en -hoeveelheid.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Markeer het evenwicht in de basisgrafiek en arceer en benoem CS en PS. Teken de lijnen niet opnieuw.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken CS, PS en TS met basis, hoogte en eenheid.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Vergelijk met de tabel bij Q=50 en Q=70 de betalingsbereidheid met de marginale kosten en geef per hoeveelheid aan of één extra transactie TS verhoogt of verlaagt.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Leg uit waarom TS in dit model maximaal is bij Q=60 en waarom dit geen oordeel over een eerlijke verdeling oplevert.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt het marktevenwicht berekenen en CS en PS grafisch herkennen.\",\n        \"Je kunt CS, PS en TS berekenen met de juiste oppervlakten en eenheden.\",\n        \"Je kunt binnen het gegeven model de aanbodlijn als marginale-kostenlijn gebruiken.\",\n        \"Je kunt met betalingsbereidheid en marginale kosten uitleggen waarom TS bij het evenwicht maximaal is, zonder daaruit een eerlijkheidsoordeel af te leiden.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A40\",\n        \"D39\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A06 and A10 are retrieval-required; PS and total-surplus operations are new formal learning here.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A40\",\n        \"D39\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Bounded inverse-supply-as-marginal-cost interpretation over a stated quantity domain.\",\n        \"D39 matches the total-surplus operation but carries a stale D28 prerequisite.\",\n        \"Total surplus maximum versus fairness boundary.\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.16\",\n        \"A2.17\",\n        \"D1.18\",\n        \"D3.1\",\n        \"D3.2\",\n        \"D3.3\",\n        \"D3.4\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Kandidaat voor circa 11 minuten. De basisgrafiek is geleverd, de aanbod-als-MK-uitspraak is tot een genoemd domein beperkt en de welvaartsconclusie is geen eerlijkheidsoordeel.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"evenwichtsvergelijking\",\n        \"markering in geleverde grafiek\",\n        \"surplusberekening\",\n        \"betalingsbereidheid-MK-vergelijking\",\n        \"begrensde conclusie\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"a\": \"50−0,5Q=5+0,25Q; 45=0,75Q; Qe=60 en Pe=€20.\",\n        \"b\": \"Horizontale as: Q (kaartjes). Verticale as: P (€ per kaartje). Markeer het snijpunt (60,20). CS ligt boven P=20 en onder de vraaglijn; PS ligt onder P=20 en boven de aanbodlijn, beide tot Q=60.\",\n        \"c\": \"CS=½×60×(50−20)=€900. PS=½×60×(20−5)=€450. TS=€900+€450=€1.350.\",\n        \"d\": \"Q=50: betalingsbereidheid=€25 en MK=€17,50, dus één extra transactie voegt €7,50 surplus toe. Q=70: betalingsbereidheid=€15 en MK=€22,50, dus die extra transactie verlaagt TS met €7,50.\",\n        \"e\": \"Tot Q=60 is de betalingsbereidheid minstens MK; daarna is de betalingsbereidheid lager dan MK. Daarom is TS binnen dit model maximaal bij Q=60. TS zegt niet hoe het surplus verdeeld is en bevat geen volledig maatschappelijk oordeel.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.3.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"2.2.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.3.3\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 3,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Pareto-efficiëntie en welvaartsverlies\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Gebruik opnieuw vraag P=50−0,5Q en aanbod P=5+0,25Q. Het vrije evenwicht is Q=60 en P=€20, met CS=€900, PS=€450 en TS=€1.350. Een kosteloos verruimbare reserveringsregel zet de prijs op €25 en beperkt boekingen tot 40 kaartjes, hoewel het platform technisch minstens 60 transacties kan verwerken. De 40 kaartjes gaan naar de vragers met de hoogste betalingsbereidheid en worden geleverd door de aanbieders met de laagste marginale kosten. Daardoor vinden precies 40 transacties plaats. Bij verruiming blijven de prijs en alle bestaande transacties ongewijzigd.\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"basisgrafiek\",\n            \"content\": \"Een assenstelsel met horizontaal Q (kaartjes) van 0 tot 100 en verticaal P (€ per kaartje) van €0 tot €50. De lijnen vraag en aanbod, het vrije evenwicht (60,20), de prijslijn P=25 en de grens Q=40 zijn getekend en gelabeld; het welvaartsverlies is nog niet gearceerd.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Omschrijf Pareto-efficiëntie binnen dit marktmodel: wanneer kan geen haalbare extra transactie of herverdeling iemand beter maken zonder iemand anders slechter te maken?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"points\": 3,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken bij P=€25 de gevraagde hoeveelheid (Qd) en aangeboden hoeveelheid (Qs). Leg met de reserveringsregel uit waarom het werkelijke aantal transacties 40 is en welke vragers en aanbieders handelen.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken bij 40 transacties en P=€25 het CS en PS. Splits elk oppervlak zo nodig in een rechthoek en een driehoek; gebruik de allocatieregel.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken TS en het welvaartsverlies ten opzichte van het vrije evenwicht. Arceer het welvaartsverlies tussen Q=40 en Q=60 in de basisgrafiek en benoem basis en hoogte.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Pas je definitie uit vraag a toe op de 41e transactie. Leg met betalingsbereidheid, marginale kosten en de kosteloze verruiming uit waarom 40 transacties niet Pareto-efficiënt zijn.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"f\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Leg uit waarom een hoger TS of Pareto-efficiëntie niet automatisch betekent dat de verdeling eerlijk is.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt Pareto-efficiëntie binnen een expliciet marktmodel definiëren en toepassen.\",\n        \"Je kunt vraag, aanbod en werkelijke transacties onder een expliciete reserverings- en allocatieregel bepalen en twee surplusstaten vergelijken.\",\n        \"Je kunt welvaartsverlies berekenen en in een geleverde grafiek arceren.\",\n        \"Je kunt efficiëntie onderscheiden van een oordeel over eerlijke verdeling.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A40\",\n        \"D20\",\n        \"D39\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A06, A10, A19, A40 and D39 are retrieval from §2.3.2.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"D20\",\n        \"Generic restriction-DWL calculation\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"A dedicated generic unit for DWL under a non-tax quantity restriction.\",\n        \"Apply a removable booking limit and highest-willingness-to-pay/lowest-marginal-cost allocation rule.\",\n        \"D20 is scoped to Pareto-efficiency in market equilibrium; this restricted-state application needs broader unit coverage.\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.16\",\n        \"A2.17\",\n        \"D1.18\",\n        \"D3.1\",\n        \"D3.2\",\n        \"D3.3\",\n        \"D3.4\",\n        \"D3.5\",\n        \"D3.6\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Kandidaat voor circa 12 minuten. De prijs, bindende reserveringsgrens, technische haalbaarheid en allocatie zijn volledig bepaald; de redundante evenwichtscontrole is vervallen.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"definitie\",\n        \"Qd-Qs-transactieketen\",\n        \"rechthoek-en-driehoek-surplus\",\n        \"TS en grafiekarcering\",\n        \"haalbare Paretoverbetering\",\n        \"eerlijkheidsgrens\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"a\": \"Pareto-efficiënt betekent hier dat geen haalbare extra transactie of herverdeling iemand beter kan maken zonder minstens één ander slechter te maken, gegeven de model- en allocatieaannames.\",\n        \"b\": \"Bij P=25: Qd=50 en Qs=80. De boekingsgrens van 40 is dus strikt lager dan vraag en aanbod. Er handelen precies de 40 vragers met de hoogste betalingsbereidheid en de 40 aanbieders met de laagste marginale kosten.\",\n        \"c\": \"Bij Q=40 is de betalingsbereidheid €30 en MK €15. CS=rechthoek 40×(30−25)=€200 plus driehoek ½×40×(50−30)=€400, dus €600. PS=rechthoek 40×(25−15)=€400 plus driehoek ½×40×(15−5)=€200, dus €600.\",\n        \"d\": \"TS=600+600=€1.200 en welvaartsverlies=1.350−1.200=€150. Het verlies is de driehoek tussen vraag en aanbod van Q=40 tot Q=60: basis 20 kaartjes, hoogte bij Q=40 is 30−15=€15; ½×20×15=€150.\",\n        \"e\": \"Bij Q=41 is de betalingsbereidheid €29,50 en zijn de marginale kosten €15,25. Een transactie tegen bijvoorbeeld €25 maakt deze vrager en aanbieder beter af, schaadt bestaande partijen niet en is technisch haalbaar na de kosteloze verruiming. Daarom is de grens van 40 niet Pareto-efficiënt.\",\n        \"f\": \"Efficiëntie gaat over onbenutte wederzijdse voordelen of totaal surplus, niet over wie het surplus ontvangt. Voor eerlijkheid is een afzonderlijk verdelingsoordeel nodig.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.3.3\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"2.2.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"module\": 2,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 4,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Gemengde opgaven: surplus en welvaart\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MIXED\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Op een eiland worden huurfietsen verhandeld. Gebruik vraag P=80−Q en aanbod P=20+0,5Q. Zonder beperking ontstaat een marktevenwicht. In het hoogseizoen zet een kosteloos verruimbare boekingsregel de transactieprijs op €45 en het aantal boekingen op maximaal 30, hoewel het platform technisch minstens 40 transacties kan verwerken. De 30 fietsen gaan naar de huurders met de hoogste betalingsbereidheid en worden aangeboden door de verhuurders met de laagste marginale kosten. Bij verruiming blijven de prijs en bestaande transacties ongewijzigd. Een bron vermeldt ook dat de meeste fietsen blauw zijn; dat gegeven is economisch niet relevant.\",\n        \"sources\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"basisgrafiek\",\n            \"content\": \"Een assenstelsel met horizontaal Q (huurfietsen) van 0 tot 80 en verticaal P (€ per huurfiets) van €0 tot €80. De lijnen vraag en aanbod zijn volledig getekend en gelabeld; het evenwicht, de prijslijn P=45, de grens Q=30 en de surplusgebieden zijn nog niet gemarkeerd.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"hulpcijfers-boeking\",\n            \"type\": \"table\",\n            \"columns\": [\n              \"toestand\",\n              \"Qd\",\n              \"Qs\",\n              \"betalingsbereidheid\",\n              \"marginale kosten\"\n            ],\n            \"rows\": [\n              [\n                \"P=€45 en Q=30\",\n                \"35\",\n                \"50\",\n                \"€50 bij Q=30\",\n                \"€35 bij Q=30\"\n              ],\n              [\n                \"mogelijke 31e transactie\",\n                \"—\",\n                \"—\",\n                \"€49\",\n                \"€35,50\"\n              ]\n            ],\n            \"content\": \"Omdat Qd=35 en Qs=50, is de boekingsgrens van 30 bindend.\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"1\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Selecteer de gegevens die nodig zijn om het vrije evenwicht te berekenen, noem het irrelevante gegeven en bereken Pe en Qe.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"2\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken bij het vrije evenwicht CS, PS en TS.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"3\",\n            \"points\": 4,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken bij 30 transacties en P=€45 met de gegeven allocatieregel CS, PS en TS. Gebruik de hulpcijfers en alleen rechthoeken en driehoeken.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"4\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Bereken het welvaartsverlies en arceer dit in de basisgrafiek. Benoem basis en hoogte; teken de vraag- en aanbodlijn niet opnieuw.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"5\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Een verhuurder zegt: ‘PS is bij de beperking hoger, dus de uitkomst is Pareto-efficiënt.’ Beoordeel deze uitspraak met beide TS-waarden en de gegeven betalingsbereidheid en marginale kosten van de 31e transactie.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"6\",\n            \"points\": 2,\n            \"prompt\": \"Leg uit waarom de efficiëntievergelijking niet bepaalt welke uitkomst eerlijker is.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"mixed_target_profile\": {\n        \"integrates_paragraphs\": [\n          \"2.3.1\",\n          \"2.3.2\",\n          \"2.3.3\"\n        ],\n        \"source_selection_required\": true,\n        \"answer_construction_required\": true,\n        \"table_or_graph_interpretation_required\": true,\n        \"no_new_theory\": true\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Je kunt uit bronnen een evenwicht bepalen en CS, PS en TS berekenen.\",\n        \"Je kunt surplus onder een expliciete boekingsgrens, prijs en allocatieregel berekenen.\",\n        \"Je kunt welvaartsverlies berekenen en in een geleverde grafiek arceren.\",\n        \"Je kunt een efficiëntieclaim beoordelen en efficiëntie onderscheiden van eerlijke verdeling.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A40\",\n        \"D20\",\n        \"D39\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"All listed operations are retrieval from §§2.3.1–2.3.3.\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"A dedicated generic unit for DWL under a non-tax quantity restriction.\",\n        \"D39 matches the total-surplus operation but carries a stale D28 prerequisite.\",\n        \"D20 is scoped to Pareto-efficiency in market equilibrium; this restricted-state application needs broader unit coverage.\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.16\",\n        \"A2.17\",\n        \"D1.18\",\n        \"D3.1\",\n        \"D3.2\",\n        \"D3.3\",\n        \"D3.4\",\n        \"D3.5\",\n        \"D3.6\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Gemengde kandidaat voor circa 18 minuten met precies zes vragen en een geleverde basisgrafiek. De boekingsgrens is strikt bindend en de opgave consolideert zonder nieuwe interventietheorie.\",\n      \"answer_form_expectations\": [\n        \"bronselectie en evenwicht\",\n        \"twee surplussets met rechthoeken en driehoeken\",\n        \"verliesberekening en arcering\",\n        \"Pareto-toepassing\",\n        \"eerlijkheidsgrens\"\n      ],\n      \"short_answer_model\": {\n        \"1\": \"Nodig: vraag- en aanbodfunctie. De kleur blauw is irrelevant. 80−Q=20+0,5Q; 60=1,5Q; Qe=40 en Pe=€40.\",\n        \"2\": \"CS=½×40×(80−40)=€800. PS=½×40×(40−20)=€400. TS=€1.200.\",\n        \"3\": \"Bij P=45 is Qd=35 en Qs=50; de grens van 30 is dus strikt bindend. Bij Q=30 is de betalingsbereidheid €50 en MK €35. CS=rechthoek 30×(50−45)=€150 plus driehoek ½×30×(80−50)=€450, dus €600. PS=rechthoek 30×(45−35)=€300 plus driehoek ½×30×(35−20)=€225, dus €525. TS=€1.125.\",\n        \"4\": \"Welvaartsverlies=1.200−1.125=€75. In de basisgrafiek: driehoek tussen vraag en aanbod van Q=30 tot Q=40; basis 10, hoogte bij Q=30 is 50−35=15; ½×10×15=75.\",\n        \"5\": \"Onjuist. PS stijgt van €400 naar €525, maar TS daalt van €1.200 naar €1.125. Bij Q=31 is de betalingsbereidheid €49 en MK €35,50; een extra transactie tegen €45 is na kosteloze verruiming haalbaar en maakt nieuwe partijen beter af zonder bestaande partijen te schaden. De uitkomst met 30 transacties is dus niet Pareto-efficiënt.\",\n        \"6\": \"De surplusberekening toont efficiëntie en verdeling van CS/PS, maar bevat geen norm voor een eerlijke verdeling. Daarvoor is een apart waardeoordeel nodig.\"\n      },\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §2.3.4\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"gemengde_opgaven\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": false,\n      \"record_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"candidate_review_ready\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": null,\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"3.1.1\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 1,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Belastingen: wig en nieuw evenwicht\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Market: Qv = −2P + 20 and Qa = P − 1. The government introduces a tax of €3 per unit, paid by the producer.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the original equilibrium price and quantity.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The tax shifts the supply curve upward by €3. Write the new supply function.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the new equilibrium. What is the consumer price (what the buyer pays)? What is the producer price (what the seller keeps after paying tax)?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the situation before and after the tax. Mark the tax wedge.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Understand that a per-unit tax creates a wedge between consumer and producer price\",\n        \"Calculate the new supply function after tax (shift supply up by tax amount)\",\n        \"Calculate the new equilibrium with tax\",\n        \"Distinguish between consumer price and producer price\",\n        \"Show the tax wedge graphically\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A23\",\n        \"A41\",\n        \"D05\",\n        \"D41\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A06\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A23\",\n        \"A41\",\n        \"D05\",\n        \"D41\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.14\",\n        \"D1.13\",\n        \"D1.24\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"The concept of 'the supply curve shifts up by the tax amount' is often taught but rarely understood. Students confuse 'upward shift = less supply.' Exercise (d) is essential — the graph makes the wedge visible. One lesson works if you focus on the mechanics (how to calculate) and save the welfare analysis for §2.3.2.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.1.1\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"2.3.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"3.1.2\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 2,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Belastingdruk en welvaartsverlies\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Using the market from §2.3.1 (original: P* = 7, Q* = 6; after tax €3: Pc = 8, Pp = 5, Qt = 3).\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"How much of the €3 tax is paid by the consumer? How much by the producer?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the afwentelingspercentage (% of tax passed on to consumer).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate CS before and after the tax.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate PS before and after the tax.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate total tax revenue for the government.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"f\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the deadweight loss. Verify: old TS = new CS + new PS + tax revenue + DWL.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Calculate how the tax burden is shared between buyer and seller\",\n        \"Calculate the afwentelingspercentage\",\n        \"Calculate CS, PS and tax revenue in the post-tax situation\",\n        \"Calculate deadweight loss from taxation\",\n        \"Understand that taxes always create some deadweight loss (except in extreme elasticity cases)\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A23\",\n        \"A32\",\n        \"A40\",\n        \"D03\",\n        \"D41\",\n        \"D42\",\n        \"D07\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A23\",\n        \"A40\",\n        \"D41\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A32\",\n        \"D03\",\n        \"D42\",\n        \"D07\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Apply the surplus-accounting identity: old TS = new CS + new PS + tax revenue + DWL (concept-level, completeness check)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.16\",\n        \"A2.17\",\n        \"D3.1\",\n        \"D3.9\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"This is a calculation-heavy lesson with 5–6 separate computations. The key insight — 'not all lost CS and PS goes to the government; some is simply lost' — is the deadweight loss. Students need the graph from §2.3.1 in front of them. One lesson is tight. Recommend: do exercises (a–d) in class, assign (e–f) as homework.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.1.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"2.3.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"3.1.3\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 3,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Subsidies\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Market: Qv = −4P + 40 and Qa = 2P − 2. The government gives a subsidy of €3 per unit to producers.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the original equilibrium.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Write the new supply function (supply shifts down by subsidy amount).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the new equilibrium. What price does the consumer pay? What does the producer effectively receive?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate CS and PS before and after the subsidy.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the total cost of the subsidy to the government (= subsidy × new Q).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"f\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the deadweight loss. (Yes, subsidies also create DWL.)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"g\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the complete graph showing CS, PS, government cost, and DWL.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Apply the tax framework in reverse: supply shifts down instead of up\",\n        \"Calculate new equilibrium, consumer and producer prices after subsidy\",\n        \"Calculate CS, PS, government expenditure, and DWL\",\n        \"Understand that subsidies also create deadweight loss (overproduction beyond efficient level)\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A27\",\n        \"A41\",\n        \"D19\",\n        \"D29\",\n        \"D43\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A41\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A27\",\n        \"D19\",\n        \"D29\",\n        \"D43\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Explain that subsidies cause DWL via overproduction beyond the Pareto-efficient quantity (concept-level, counterintuitive to students)\",\n        \"Compute government subsidy expenditure = subsidy × Q_new (reken skill, A-domain candidate)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.15\",\n        \"A2.16\",\n        \"D3.5\",\n        \"D3.9\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"If students understood §2.3.1 and §2.3.2, this is mechanical transfer — same skills, different direction. The surprise is that subsidies also cause DWL (students assume subsidies are 'purely good'). One lesson works because the method is identical to taxes.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.1.3\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"2.3.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"3.1.4\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 4,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Maximumprijs\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"The market for rental housing: Qv = −5P + 6000 and Qa = 3P − 1600 (P in €/month, Q in apartments). The government sets a maximum rent of €800/month.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the equilibrium rent and number of apartments rented.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The government sets a maximum rent of €800/month. Is this below or above the equilibrium price? Why does this matter?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the quantity demanded and quantity supplied at P = 800. Calculate the shortage.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the graph. Shade the shortage.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate CS and PS at the maximum price. Calculate the deadweight loss.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"f\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A student says: 'The maximum price is good because it helps renters.' Evaluate this claim: which renters benefit, and who is harmed?\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Understand that a maximum price must be below equilibrium to have effect\",\n        \"Calculate shortage = Qd − Qs at the controlled price\",\n        \"Calculate welfare effects: some CS is gained from lower price, some is lost from fewer transactions\",\n        \"Identify deadweight loss graphically and computationally\",\n        \"Evaluate the trade-off: lower price for some vs fewer available units\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A26\",\n        \"A32\",\n        \"D34\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A26\",\n        \"A32\",\n        \"D34\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Structure a beoordeel-answer: who wins, who loses under a max price — renters-with-a-unit vs renters-without (concept-level + verbal skill)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.15\",\n        \"A2.16\",\n        \"D3.10\",\n        \"D3.9\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"The welfare analysis here is more nuanced than taxes: some consumers gain, others lose access entirely. Exercise (f) forces critical thinking. One lesson works — the graph is similar to §2.2.3 (forced price deviation), so students recognise the pattern.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.1.4\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"2.4.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"3.1.5\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 5,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Minimumprijs en quota\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"The EU sets a minimum price for butter at €6/kg. The market: Qv = −10P + 100 and Qa = 5P − 5 (Q in millions of kg).\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the equilibrium price and quantity.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate Qd and Qa at the minimum price of €6. Calculate the surplus (aanbodoverschot).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The EU buys the surplus. Calculate the cost to the EU.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the graph. Shade the surplus, CS, PS, and the deadweight loss.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Who benefits from the minimum price? Who pays? Is it Pareto-efficient?\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Understand that a minimum price must be above equilibrium to have effect\",\n        \"Calculate surplus = Qs − Qd at the controlled price\",\n        \"Understand government options: buy surplus, destroy it, or export it\",\n        \"Calculate the cost of intervention\",\n        \"Calculate welfare effects: PS increases but CS decreases, plus DWL\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A25\",\n        \"A32\",\n        \"D34\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"D34\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A25\",\n        \"A32\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Compute the cost to the government of buying up the aanbodoverschot = (Qs − Qd) × Pmin (reken skill, A-domain candidate)\",\n        \"Evaluate distributional effect: who gains (producers), who pays (consumers + taxpayers) (concept-level + verbal)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.15\",\n        \"A2.16\",\n        \"D3.10\",\n        \"D3.9\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Mirror of §2.4.1. Students who understood maximum price handle this quickly. The surplus (aanbodoverschot) vs shortage distinction is important to drill. The EU butter/milk example makes it concrete. One lesson comfortable.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.1.5\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"placeholder_reason\": \"Merged or related v4 records require review: 2.4.3.\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"2.4.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [\n          \"2.4.3\"\n        ],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"difficulty\": \"MIXED\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"placeholder\": true,\n        \"context\": \"Placeholder for 3.1.6: integrated practice across this chapter. It consolidates earlier theory and introduces no new theory.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Combine at least two earlier skills from this chapter in one exam-style context.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Consolidate and transfer the chapter skills for Gemengde opgaven: overheidsingrijpen.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"v5_placeholder_needs_review\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Count-bearing consolidation paragraph. It introduces no new theory and needs a reviewed integration target exercise.\",\n      \"id\": \"3.1.6\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 6,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Gemengde opgaven: overheidsingrijpen\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"gemengde_opgaven\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": false,\n      \"record_status\": \"placeholder_needs_review\",\n      \"placeholder_reason\": \"Gemengde-opgaven target exercise must be designed as integration/transfer practice.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.1.6\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"placeholder_needs_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": null,\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"3.2.1\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 1,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Volkomen concurrentie: kenmerken\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"LIGHT\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"A wheat farmer sells wheat at the market price of €200 per ton. He cannot influence this price.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Name four characteristics of a perfectly competitive market. For each, explain why the wheat market fits.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The farmer considers charging €210 per ton. Explain why this won't work.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the farmer's individual price line. Label it P = GO = MO.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Explain why for this farmer MO = price.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Name the four characteristics: many buyers and sellers, homogeneous product, free entry/exit, perfect information\",\n        \"Explain why individual firms are price takers (hoeveelheidsaanpasser)\",\n        \"Understand that P = GO = MO in perfect competition (horizontal price line)\",\n        \"Draw the individual firm's price/revenue lines\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"D09\",\n        \"D15\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"D09\",\n        \"D15\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Name the four characteristics of volkomen concurrentie and recognise them in a concrete market (concept-level, mastery_target understand)\",\n        \"Explain why an individual firm in volkomen concurrentie is a hoeveelheidsaanpasser / price taker (concept-level)\",\n        \"Show P = GO = MO for the individual firm (grafisch + concept; the horizontal price line representation)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"D2.1\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Conceptual lesson. The P = GO = MO equality is stated and understood visually, not derived mathematically. Students find this intuitive ('if you always get the same price, each extra unit brings in that price'). One lesson comfortable — use the extra time for real-world examples (commodity markets, agriculture).\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.2.1\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.1.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"3.2.2\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 2,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Winstmaximalisatie bij volkomen concurrentie\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"The wheat farmer's costs are TK = 0.5Q² + 10Q + 500 (Q in tons). Market price is €200/ton. First use of the differentiation power rule on a quadratic TK.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Write down the MK function. (Hint: differentiate TK. Remember: if TK = aQ² + bQ + c, then MK = 2aQ + b.)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Write down the MO. (What is MO for a price taker?)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Set MO = MK and solve for Q. This is the profit-maximising quantity.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate total revenue, total costs, and total profit at this Q.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw a graph with MK and the horizontal price/MO line. Mark the profit-maximising point.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"f\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Would this farmer produce at Q = 100? Explain using MO and MK.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Differentiate a simple quadratic TK function to find MK (power rule: bring down exponent, reduce by one)\",\n        \"State MO = P for a price-taking firm\",\n        \"Find profit-maximising output by setting MO = MK\",\n        \"Calculate winst at optimal Q\",\n        \"Show the MO = MK solution graphically\",\n        \"Explain why producing beyond MO = MK reduces profit\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A11\",\n        \"A13\",\n        \"A94\",\n        \"A21\",\n        \"A33\",\n        \"D30\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A21\",\n        \"D30\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A11\",\n        \"A13\",\n        \"A94\",\n        \"A33\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Apply the power rule to differentiate aQ² + bQ + c, producing 2aQ + b (A-domain candidate; A11 covers 'Afgeleide bepalen' generally but not this specific TK→MK recipe)\",\n        \"State MO = P as a special case for a price-taker firm (concept-level, tight pair with §3.1.1)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.11\",\n        \"A2.12\",\n        \"D1.19\",\n        \"D1.21\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"This is where differentiation enters the course. The power rule on a quadratic is simple ('multiply by 2, drop the Q's power by 1'). Students need to see it as a recipe. The conceptual link ('marginal cost tells you what one more ton costs') was established in §1.4.3–§4. Exercise (f) tests whether they understand the rule, not just apply it. One lesson is tight — spend the first 15 minutes on the differentiation technique, then apply it. An algebra bridge at the end of the lesson previews GTK = TK/Q term-by-term for §3.1.3.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.2.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"placeholder_reason\": \"Merged or related v4 records require review: 1.4.4.\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.1.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [\n          \"1.4.4\"\n        ],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"3.2.3\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 3,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Langetermijnevenwicht\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"The wheat farmer from §3.1.2 makes a profit. Students walk the long-run dynamics: entry → supply shifts → price falls → profit erodes → zero-profit equilibrium at P = min GTK = MK.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Explain what will happen in the long run: will new farmers enter or exit the market? What happens to total market supply?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Show in a market diagram (supply and demand) what happens to the market price when supply increases.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"At what profit level will entry stop? Explain.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d1\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Schrijf GTK = TK/Q op. Deel elke term van TK = 0,5Q² + 10Q + 500 door Q. (Verwacht resultaat: GTK = 0,5Q + 10 + 500/Q.)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d2\",\n            \"prompt\": \"In long-run equilibrium, price equals the minimum of GTK. Set GTK = MK (= Q + 10) and solve for Q. (Tip: trek 10 van beide kanten af, vermenigvuldig met Q om de breuk weg te werken, verzamel Q²-termen.) Dit is de efficiënte schaal van het bedrijf.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate GTK at the Q you found in (d2). This is the long-run equilibrium price. Verify that profit = 0 at this price.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"f\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the long-run equilibrium for the individual firm: P = GTK at the minimum of GTK, with the MK curve passing through that point. Explain why this is the long-run result.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Explain that positive profit attracts entry; negative profit causes exit\",\n        \"Understand that entry shifts market supply right → price falls\",\n        \"Understand that in long-run equilibrium: economic profit = 0\",\n        \"Herleid GTK uit TK door term-voor-term delen door Q\",\n        \"Calculate the efficient scale by setting GTK = MK and solving for Q\",\n        \"Calculate the long-run equilibrium price from the minimum GTK\",\n        \"Show that long-run equilibrium occurs where P = minimum GTK = MK\",\n        \"Draw the individual firm's long-run equilibrium graph\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A11\",\n        \"A13\",\n        \"A14\",\n        \"A28\",\n        \"A37\",\n        \"D13\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A11\",\n        \"A13\",\n        \"A14\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A28\",\n        \"A37\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Herleid GTK uit TK door term-voor-term delen door Q (A-domain candidate; the 500/Q-term is the didactic bottleneck explicitly scaffolded into §3.1.2)\",\n        \"Argue that entry erodes positive profit and exit eliminates losses, so long-run profit = 0 in volkomen concurrentie (concept-level, mastery_target understand)\",\n        \"Recognise the long-run equilibrium geometric condition P = min GTK = MK and explain why (concept-level + grafisch)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.11\",\n        \"A4.5\",\n        \"D1.21\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Conceptual reasoning about dynamics plus a meaningful calculation. Exercises (d1)/(d2) are the key new skill: herleiden van GTK en vervolgens GTK = MK oplossen. The 500/Q term is demanding; the scaffold (d1→d2) and the algebra bridge at the end of §3.1.2 are cruciaal — zonder die stappen wordt deze paragraaf overweldigend voor de onderkant van de klas.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.2.3\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.1.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"difficulty\": \"MIXED\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"placeholder\": true,\n        \"context\": \"Placeholder for 3.2.4: integrated practice across this chapter. It consolidates earlier theory and introduces no new theory.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Combine at least two earlier skills from this chapter in one exam-style context.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Consolidate and transfer the chapter skills for Gemengde opgaven: volkomen concurrentie.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"v5_placeholder_needs_review\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Count-bearing consolidation paragraph. It introduces no new theory and needs a reviewed integration target exercise.\",\n      \"id\": \"3.2.4\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 4,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Gemengde opgaven: volkomen concurrentie\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"gemengde_opgaven\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": false,\n      \"record_status\": \"placeholder_needs_review\",\n      \"placeholder_reason\": \"Gemengde-opgaven target exercise must be designed as integration/transfer practice.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.2.4\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"placeholder_needs_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": null,\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"3.3.1\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 1,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Monopolie: kenmerken\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"The NS (Dutch Railways) is the only provider of train services on most routes. Students map causes of monopoly, the prijszetter role, and compare with volkomen concurrentie.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Name three reasons why monopolies can exist (with an example for each).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A monopolist faces the entire market demand curve. Explain why a monopolist is a prijszetter, not a hoeveelheidsaanpasser.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Compare perfect competition and monopoly in a table: number of sellers, product type, entry barriers, price influence.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Apple charges €1200 for an iPhone while production costs are estimated at €400. Does Apple have a monopoly? Explain using market structure characteristics.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Name causes of monopoly: legal (government-granted), natural (economies of scale), patents\",\n        \"Explain that a monopolist faces the downward-sloping market demand curve\",\n        \"Understand prijszetter: the monopolist chooses the price (or equivalently, the quantity)\",\n        \"Distinguish monopoly from perfect competition using structural characteristics\",\n        \"Apply the concept to real-world cases (critical thinking: is Apple really a monopolist?)\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"D15\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"D15\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Name the typical causes of monopoly — legal, natural, patent — with an example each (concept-level, mastery_target understand)\",\n        \"Contrast prijszetter vs hoeveelheidsaanpasser and link to the slope of the firm-level demand curve (concept-level)\",\n        \"Judge real-world market power claims against the four structural characteristics (concept-level + verbal analysis; the 'is Apple really a monopolist?' style question)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"D2.1\",\n        \"D2.2\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Conceptual lesson with no calculations. Exercise (d) is a discussion-starter — Apple has market power but isn't a pure monopolist (substitutes exist). This nuance engages students. One lesson comfortable.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.3.1\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.2.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"3.3.2\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 2,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Marginale opbrengst bij monopolie\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"A monopolist faces demand Qv = 100 − 2P, or equivalently P = 50 − 0.5Q. Students build the Q/P/TO/MO table, then verify with differentiation.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Complete the Q/P/TO/MO table for Q = 0, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60 using TO = P×Q and MO = ΔTO/ΔQ.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"At which Q is TO maximised? What is MO at that point?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the demand curve (P line) and the MO curve in the same graph. What do you notice about the slope of MO compared to the demand curve?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Explain in words why MO is lower than the price for a monopolist. (Hint: to sell one more unit, the monopolist must lower the price on ALL units.)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Verify using the formula: TO = P × Q = (50 − 0.5Q) × Q = 50Q − 0.5Q². Differentiate to find MO. Compare with your table.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Calculate TO from a demand function: TO = P × Q\",\n        \"Calculate MO from a table (ΔTO/ΔQ)\",\n        \"Discover that MO < P for a monopolist (because lowering price on all units)\",\n        \"Observe that the MO curve has twice the slope of the demand curve\",\n        \"Derive MO by differentiating TO (first application of power rule on a quadratic revenue function)\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A03\",\n        \"A07\",\n        \"A11\",\n        \"A12\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A03\",\n        \"A07\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A11\",\n        \"A12\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Explain why MO < P for a monopolist — the 'price-cut-on-all-units' argument (concept-level, the non-obvious insight of this paragraph)\",\n        \"Observe that the MO curve has twice the slope of a linear demand curve (concept-level + grafisch; pattern recognition before calculus)\",\n        \"Build TO = P×Q from a linear P(Q) and expand into aQ + bQ² form (A-domain candidate; step-0 of the §3.2.3 procedure)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.11\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"This is the key preparation lesson for §3.2.3. The table method (exercises a–c) builds intuition BEFORE the algebra (exercise e). Students see the pattern in the numbers first, then verify with calculus. The differentiation in (e) uses the same power rule from §3.1.2. One lesson works IF you do the table first — don't start with algebra.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.3.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.2.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"3.3.3\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 3,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Winstmaximalisatie bij monopolie\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"HEAVY\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"The monopolist from §3.2.2 has demand P = 50 − 0.5Q and costs TK = 0.25Q² + 5Q + 100. The hardest single lesson in the course: the full 6-step monopoly procedure from demand to P*, Q*, winst and graph.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Write TO as a function of Q: TO = (50 − 0.5Q) × Q. Expand.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Differentiate TO to find MO.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Differentiate TK to find MK.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Set MO = MK. Solve for Q.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Find the price by substituting Q back into the demand function P = 50 − 0.5Q.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"f\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate total profit: Winst = TO − TK.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"g\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the graph: demand curve, MO, MK. Mark the profit-maximising Q and P. Shade the area of profit.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"h\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Could the monopolist charge a higher price than your answer in (e)? What would happen to quantity and profit?\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Execute the full monopoly profit maximisation procedure: TO→MO, TK→MK, MO=MK, substitute back, winst\",\n        \"Draw the complete monopoly graph with MO, MK, demand curve\",\n        \"Identify the profit area graphically\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A03\",\n        \"A07\",\n        \"A11\",\n        \"A12\",\n        \"A13\",\n        \"A20\",\n        \"A21\",\n        \"A35\",\n        \"A40\",\n        \"D30\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A03\",\n        \"A07\",\n        \"A11\",\n        \"A12\",\n        \"A13\",\n        \"A20\",\n        \"A21\",\n        \"A40\",\n        \"D30\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A35\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Execute the full 6-step monopoly procedure end-to-end (A35 is registered as 'Max. winst monopolist'; this procedure-execution skill is what it should canonically encode — verify A35's procedure field covers it)\",\n        \"Explain why charging above P* reduces profit — the demand constraint (concept-level, verbal)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.11\",\n        \"A2.12\",\n        \"D1.21\",\n        \"D2.2\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"The most demanding lesson in the course. 6 algebraic steps plus a graph. However, students have been prepared: MK differentiation (§3.1.2), MO table and differentiation (§3.2.2), MO = MK rule (§1.4.3–§4). The exercise is scaffolded step-by-step. In class: work through steps (a–f) together as guided practice, assign (g–h) as homework. One lesson is tight but feasible with this scaffolding.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.3.3\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.2.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"difficulty\": \"MIXED\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"placeholder\": true,\n        \"context\": \"Placeholder for 3.3.4: integrated practice across this chapter. It consolidates earlier theory and introduces no new theory.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Combine at least two earlier skills from this chapter in one exam-style context.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Consolidate and transfer the chapter skills for Gemengde opgaven: monopolie.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"v5_placeholder_needs_review\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Count-bearing consolidation paragraph. It introduces no new theory and needs a reviewed integration target exercise.\",\n      \"id\": \"3.3.4\",\n      \"module\": 3,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 4,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Gemengde opgaven: monopolie\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"gemengde_opgaven\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": false,\n      \"record_status\": \"placeholder_needs_review\",\n      \"placeholder_reason\": \"Gemengde-opgaven target exercise must be designed as integration/transfer practice.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §3.3.4\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"placeholder_needs_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": null,\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.1.1\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 1,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Welvaartseffecten van monopolie\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Using the monopolist from §3.2.3 (optimal P = €30, Q = 40, demand P = 50 − 0.5Q, MK = 0.5Q + 5). Compare monopoly welfare to the competitive benchmark (P = MK) and compute the Harberger triangle.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate CS at the monopoly price. (Triangle between demand curve and price, from Q = 0 to Q = 40.)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate PS at the monopoly price.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Now calculate what TS would be under perfect competition (where P = MK, i.e. demand = MK). Find the competitive P and Q. Calculate competitive TS.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the deadweight loss (Harberger triangle) = competitive TS − monopoly TS.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Shade the Harberger triangle in your graph. Explain in words: 'where did this surplus go?'\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Calculate CS and PS under monopoly pricing\",\n        \"Calculate the competitive outcome (P = MK) as benchmark\",\n        \"Calculate the Harberger triangle (deadweight loss from monopoly)\",\n        \"Understand that monopoly transfers surplus from consumers to producer AND destroys some surplus entirely\",\n        \"Explain why monopoly is not Pareto-efficient\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A30\",\n        \"A32\",\n        \"A35\",\n        \"A40\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A30\",\n        \"A32\",\n        \"A35\",\n        \"A40\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Construct a competitive benchmark by imposing P = MK and computing P*, Q*, TS (concept-level + application; the §3.3.1 novelty is the comparison, not the components)\",\n        \"Distinguish surplus transfer (CS→PS) from surplus destruction (DWL) verbally (concept-level, the 'where did the surplus go?' narrative)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.16\",\n        \"A2.17\",\n        \"D3.1\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Builds directly on §2.2 (surplus analysis) and §3.2.3 (monopoly mechanics). The calculation involves finding the competitive equilibrium (demand = supply/MK) — which is revision of earlier competitive-equilibrium work. The new element is the comparison. One lesson works because students already have all the component skills.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.1.1\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.3.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.1.2\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 2,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Prijsdiscriminatie\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"A theme park faces two groups: adults (Qv = −5P + 300) and children (Qv = −10P + 300). MK = €10 (constant). Combined demand is given as Qv = −15P + 600.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Without price discrimination: the combined demand curve is given as Qv = −15P + 600. Set MO = MK, find optimal P and Q. Calculate total profit.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"With price discrimination: the park charges adults and children different prices. For each group separately, derive MO, set MO = MK and find optimal P and Q. Calculate total profit.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"How much extra profit does price discrimination yield?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"What conditions must be met for price discrimination to work? (Hint: can adults buy children's tickets?)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Name a real-world example of price discrimination and explain how the conditions are met.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Define price discrimination: charging different prices to different groups for the same product\",\n        \"Calculate profit with and without price discrimination\",\n        \"Understand that price discrimination increases producer surplus\",\n        \"Name conditions: market segments must be separable, no resale possible\",\n        \"Recognise real-world examples (student discounts, airline pricing, happy hour)\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A11\",\n        \"A91\",\n        \"A35\",\n        \"A36\",\n        \"D18\",\n        \"D21\",\n        \"D22\",\n        \"D24\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A11\",\n        \"A91\",\n        \"A35\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A36\",\n        \"D18\",\n        \"D21\",\n        \"D22\",\n        \"D24\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Apply the MO=MK procedure twice (once per market segment) and sum the resulting profits (A-domain candidate; a 'two-market monopoly' variant)\",\n        \"Compare winst with vs without price discrimination and quantify the extra PS captured (reken skill)\",\n        \"Name and verify the three operational conditions for price discrimination: separable segments, no resale, different elasticities (concept-level; partly covered by D24 but worth a procedure check)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.11\",\n        \"D2.3\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"The combined demand curve is provided in exercise (a) to avoid the aggregation complexity. Exercise (b) applies the MO = MK procedure twice (once per group), which is repetitive but reinforcing. One lesson works with this simplification.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.1.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.3.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.1.3\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 3,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Marktvormen vergelijken\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Descriptive/comparative lesson: build the 4-structure comparison table and classify real-world markets.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Complete the comparison table (rows: number of sellers, product type, entry barriers, price influence, example) across columns: volkomen concurrentie, monopolistische concurrentie, oligopolie, monopolie.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A coffee shop in Amsterdam operates in a market with many competitors, but each shop has its own style and menu. Which market structure is this? Explain.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The petrol market has 4 major companies. Products are nearly identical. Which market structure? Explain.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Rank the four market structures from most to least competitive. For each, state whether TS is higher or lower than at volkomen concurrentie.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Why might an oligopolist have an incentive to collude (prijsafspraken maken)? Why is this illegal?\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Name and characterise all four market structures\",\n        \"Classify real-world markets into the correct structure\",\n        \"Understand the spectrum from perfect competition to monopoly\",\n        \"Understand that market power generally reduces total surplus\",\n        \"Recognise collusion as an attempt to act as a collective monopolist\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"D15\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"D15\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Characterise monopolistische concurrentie (many sellers, productdifferentiatie, free entry, limited short-run price-setting power) (concept-level, mastery_target understand)\",\n        \"Characterise oligopolie (few sellers, strategic interaction, high entry barriers, price leadership / collusion risk) (concept-level, mastery_target understand)\",\n        \"Classify a given real-world market into one of the four structures using the characteristic table (concept-level application)\",\n        \"Explain the incentive to collude in oligopolie and why cartels are illegal + unstable (concept-level; hooks into the §3.3.3 Gap 1 revision plan)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"D2.1\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Descriptive/comparative lesson. The table is a study tool students can use for revision. Exercise (e) bridges to game theory (which is covered elsewhere in the full VWO program). One lesson comfortable — good opportunity for class discussion about real-world examples.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.1.3\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.3.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.1.4\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 4,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Negatieve externe effecten\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"A factory produces chemicals. Private marginal costs are MPC = 2Q. Production causes water pollution costing fishermen €4 per unit, so MSC = 2Q + 4. Demand P = 20 − Q.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the market equilibrium (where demand = MPC). This is what the market produces without government intervention.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the socially optimal equilibrium (where demand = MSC).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw both equilibria in one graph. Shade the welfare loss from overproduction.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"How many units too many does the free market produce?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Explain why this is called market failure. Who bears the cost that the market ignores?\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Define external effects (externalities): costs/benefits affecting third parties not involved in the transaction\",\n        \"Distinguish private costs from social costs: MSC = MPC + external cost\",\n        \"Calculate market equilibrium (using MPC) and socially optimal equilibrium (using MSC)\",\n        \"Calculate the welfare loss from overproduction (the market produces too much)\",\n        \"Explain why externalities cause market failure: the market price doesn't reflect the full cost\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"F07\",\n        \"F08\",\n        \"F16\",\n        \"F17\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"F07\",\n        \"F08\",\n        \"F16\",\n        \"F17\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Verbal market-failure framing: who bears the ignored cost, and why does the market price undercount it (concept-level)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.16\",\n        \"A2.17\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"New graph type: two supply curves (MPC and MSC). Students must understand why the market uses MPC (because firms don't pay for pollution) while society should use MSC. The math is straightforward — it's the same equilibrium calculation introduced earlier, just done twice. One lesson works.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.1.4\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.4.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.1.5\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 5,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Positieve externe effecten\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Vaccinations have positive external effects. MPB curve (private benefit) P = 30 − Q; MSB = MPB + €5 = 35 − Q. Supply (= MC) P = Q.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the market equilibrium (where supply = MPB).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the socially optimal equilibrium (where supply = MSB). To do this: MSB = 35 − Q. Set equal to supply.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The market produces too little. By how many units?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw both equilibria. Shade the welfare loss from underproduction.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Suggest a policy to correct this market failure. How large should the correction be?\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Distinguish private benefits from social benefits: MSB = MPB + external benefit\",\n        \"Calculate market equilibrium (using MPB) and socially optimal equilibrium (using MSB)\",\n        \"Understand that the market produces too little of goods with positive externalities\",\n        \"Suggest correction: subsidy equal to the external benefit per unit\",\n        \"Draw the graph with MPB and MSB\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"F11\",\n        \"F16\",\n        \"F17\",\n        \"F18\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"F16\",\n        \"F17\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"F11\",\n        \"F18\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.16\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Mirror of §3.4.1 — if students understood negative externalities, this is the same logic reversed. The policy suggestion (exercise e) connects back to §2.3 (subsidies). One lesson comfortable.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.1.5\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.4.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.1.6\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 6,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Overheidsingrijpen bij marktfalen\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Using the factory from §3.4.1 (MPC = 2Q, MSC = 2Q + 4, demand P = 20 − Q). Students derive the Pigouvian tax, meet Coase, and revisit patents as a dual-edged tool.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The government introduces a Pigouvian tax of €4 per unit. Write the new private cost function (MPC + tax). Calculate the new market equilibrium.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Compare this with the socially optimal outcome from §3.4.1(b). Are they the same?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"An economist suggests: 'Instead of a tax, let the fishermen negotiate with the factory. If they pay the factory to reduce output, both sides can be better off.' This is called the Coase theorem. Under what condition does this work? (Hint: think about transaction costs and property rights.)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The factory has a patent on its chemical process. Explain how a patent can simultaneously be a solution to one market failure (innovation) and a cause of another (monopoly). Connect to §3.2.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Name three policy instruments the government can use to correct negative externalities and give a real example for each.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Understand Pigouvian tax: a tax equal to the external cost per unit\",\n        \"Show that a Pigouvian tax makes the market outcome equal to the socially optimal outcome\",\n        \"Understand the Coase theorem: private negotiation can solve externalities if transaction costs are low\",\n        \"Recognise the trade-off in patents: innovation incentive vs monopoly power\",\n        \"Name policy instruments: taxes, subsidies, regulation, tradeable permits, property rights\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A23\",\n        \"F05\",\n        \"F06\",\n        \"F10\",\n        \"F18\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A23\",\n        \"F18\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"F05\",\n        \"F06\",\n        \"F10\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"State the Coase theorem condition (low transaction costs, clear property rights) and argue when private negotiation suffices (concept-level, mastery_target understand)\",\n        \"Evaluate the patent trade-off: innovation incentive vs monopoly welfare loss (concept-level + verbal; the §3.4.3↔§3.2 integration)\",\n        \"Enumerate policy instruments for negative externalities with a real example each (concept-level, breadth)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"D3.4\",\n        \"D3.9\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Mix of calculation (a–b) and conceptual reasoning (c–e). The Coase theorem can be kept light — just the basic idea, not formal proof. Exercise (d) is an excellent integrative question connecting market failure to monopoly. One lesson works — the calculation is revision, the new elements are Coase and policy instruments.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.1.6\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"3.4.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"difficulty\": \"MIXED\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"placeholder\": true,\n        \"context\": \"Placeholder for 4.1.7: integrated practice across this chapter. It consolidates earlier theory and introduces no new theory.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Combine at least two earlier skills from this chapter in one exam-style context.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Consolidate and transfer the chapter skills for Gemengde opgaven: marktvormen en marktfalen.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"v5_placeholder_needs_review\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Count-bearing consolidation paragraph. It introduces no new theory and needs a reviewed integration target exercise.\",\n      \"id\": \"4.1.7\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 1,\n      \"paragraph\": 7,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Gemengde opgaven: marktvormen en marktfalen\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"gemengde_opgaven\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": false,\n      \"record_status\": \"placeholder_needs_review\",\n      \"placeholder_reason\": \"Gemengde-opgaven target exercise must be designed as integration/transfer practice.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.1.7\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"placeholder_needs_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": null,\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.2.1\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 1,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Arbeidsvraag\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"LIGHT\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"A restaurant hires waiters. Marginal product falls: 1st=50 extra meals, 2nd=45, 3rd=35, 4th=25, 5th=15. Revenue is €15 per meal. Students compute VMP and apply the hiring rule VMP ≥ W.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the value of marginal product (VMP = marginal product × revenue per meal) for each waiter.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The wage is €450 per evening. How many waiters should the restaurant hire? Explain using the VMP.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The wage rises to €600. How many waiters now? What happened to labor demand?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Explain why the demand for labor is called 'derived demand.'\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the restaurant's labor demand curve using the VMP values.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Define marginal product of labor (extra output from one additional worker)\",\n        \"Calculate value of marginal product: VMP = MP × price of output\",\n        \"Apply the hiring rule: hire as long as VMP ≥ wage\",\n        \"Understand derived demand: labor is demanded because of demand for the product\",\n        \"Draw a labor demand curve (which is the VMP curve)\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"L01\",\n        \"L02\",\n        \"L03\",\n        \"L04\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"L01\",\n        \"L02\",\n        \"L03\",\n        \"L04\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Connects directly to earlier marginal-analysis units (MO = MK is analogous to VMP = W). Students find the restaurant context engaging. The diminishing marginal product is intuitive ('the kitchen gets crowded'). One lesson comfortable.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.2.1\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"4.1.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.2.2\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 2,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Arbeidsaanbod en participatie\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Country X: population 15–75 = 12 million; beroepsbevolking = 9 million; werkloze beroepsbevolking = 540,000.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the bruto participatiegraad (participation rate).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the werkloosheidspercentage (unemployment rate).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A student says: '3 million people are unemployed because 12 − 9 = 3 million.' Explain why this is wrong.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Average wages rise significantly. Explain how this might affect the participation rate (think about people currently in the niet-beroepsbevolking).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw a labor supply curve. Explain why it is upward sloping.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Define beroepsbevolking, niet-beroepsbevolking, werkloze beroepsbevolking\",\n        \"Calculate participation rate = beroepsbevolking / population 15–75 × 100\",\n        \"Calculate unemployment rate = werklozen / beroepsbevolking × 100\",\n        \"Explain what drives labor supply: wages, preferences, demographics\",\n        \"Draw an upward-sloping labor supply curve\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"L05\",\n        \"L06\",\n        \"L07\",\n        \"L08\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"L05\",\n        \"L06\",\n        \"L07\",\n        \"L08\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"The terminology (beroepsbevolking vs niet-beroepsbevolking) is the main challenge — students mix up 'not working' with 'unemployed.' Exercise (c) directly addresses this confusion. The formulas are simple. One lesson works with clear emphasis on definitions.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.2.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"4.1.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.2.3\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 3,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Evenwicht op de arbeidsmarkt\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"LIGHT\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"The labor market for IT workers: Qd = −200W + 5000 and Qs = 300W − 1000 (W = wage €/hour, Q = number of workers).\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the equilibrium wage and employment level.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"At a wage of €14/hour, is there a krappe arbeidsmarkt or a ruime arbeidsmarkt? Calculate the gap.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"In a krappe arbeidsmarkt, what happens to wages? In a ruime arbeidsmarkt? Explain.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the labor market diagram with the equilibrium and the situation at W = €14.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"New tech companies enter the market, increasing demand for IT workers. The new demand is Qd = −200W + 7000. Calculate the new equilibrium. What happened to wages and employment?\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Apply supply-demand equilibrium framework to the labor market\",\n        \"Calculate equilibrium wage and employment\",\n        \"Define krappe arbeidsmarkt (demand > supply) and ruime arbeidsmarkt (supply > demand)\",\n        \"Predict wage adjustments from market imbalances\",\n        \"Analyse shifts in labor demand or supply\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A02\",\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A06\",\n        \"L09\",\n        \"L10\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A02\",\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A06\",\n        \"L10\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"L09\",\n        \"L10\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.10\",\n        \"A2.12\",\n        \"A2.15\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Direct transfer from §1.4 — same math, different context. Students recognise the framework. Exercise (e) mirrors §1.4.2. One lesson comfortable. This is a confidence-building lesson.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.2.3\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"4.1.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.2.4\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 4,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Werkloosheid\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Classify five real-world cases as conjuncturele werkloosheid, structurele werkloosheid, or frictiewerkloosheid and propose policy responses.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A coal miner in Limburg loses his job because the mine closes permanently due to the shift to renewable energy. Which type of unemployment?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A marketing manager quits her job to search for a better position. She is unemployed for 3 months. Which type?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A construction worker is laid off because the housing market crashed during a recession. Which type?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Factory workers are replaced by robots. Their skills don't match available jobs. Which type?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"For each type, explain whether it is temporary or can persist for a long time.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"f\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A government wants to reduce structurele werkloosheid. Name two policy measures and explain how they work.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Define three types of unemployment: conjunctureel (cyclical), structureel (structural), frictie (frictional)\",\n        \"Classify real-world cases into the correct type\",\n        \"Understand causes: demand shortfall (cyclical), skills mismatch or automation (structural), search time (frictional)\",\n        \"Suggest policy responses for each type\",\n        \"Understand that frictional unemployment is normal and even healthy\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"L11\",\n        \"L12\",\n        \"L13\",\n        \"L14\",\n        \"L15\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"L11\",\n        \"L12\",\n        \"L13\",\n        \"L14\",\n        \"L15\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Definitional lesson. The classification can be ambiguous (is the coal miner structural or frictional?) — this makes for good discussion. Exercise (f) requires policy thinking. One lesson works.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.2.4\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"4.2.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.2.5\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 5,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Minimumloon\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"IT labor market from §4.1.3 (Qd = −200W + 5000, Qs = 300W − 1000, W* = €12, Q* = 2600). Government sets a minimum wage of €15/hour.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate Qd and Qs at the minimum wage of €15/hour.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"How many workers become unemployed? (= Qs − Qd)\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Draw the labor market with the minimum wage. Shade the werknemerssurplus and werkgeverssurplus.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the total surplus before and after the minimum wage.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the deadweight loss.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"f\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Some workers earn more (€15 instead of €12), but others lose their jobs. Evaluate: 'A minimum wage helps workers.' Use your calculations to argue both sides.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Apply the minimum price framework from §2.4.2 to the labor market\",\n        \"Calculate unemployment caused by a minimum wage (surplus of labor)\",\n        \"Calculate welfare effects: werknemerssurplus, werkgeverssurplus, deadweight loss\",\n        \"Recognise the trade-off: higher wages for employed vs job losses for others\",\n        \"Formulate a balanced evaluation of minimum wage policy\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A02\",\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A25\",\n        \"A32\",\n        \"D16\",\n        \"H13\",\n        \"H14\",\n        \"L10\",\n        \"L16\",\n        \"L21\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A02\",\n        \"A04\",\n        \"A06\",\n        \"A10\",\n        \"A19\",\n        \"A25\",\n        \"A32\",\n        \"L10\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"D16\",\n        \"H13\",\n        \"H14\",\n        \"L16\",\n        \"L21\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.16\",\n        \"A4.5\",\n        \"D3.10\",\n        \"H5.2\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"This is a direct transfer from Module 2 (minimumprijs). Students should recognise the framework: a minimum wage IS a minimum price on labor. The welfare calculation is identical. The new element is exercise (f) — a balanced evaluation — which is an exam skill (standpuntbepaling). One lesson works. If there is a long time gap between Module 2 and this lesson, start with a 10-minute revision of minimum price mechanics.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.2.5\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"4.2.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.2.6\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 6,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Vakbonden, cao en arbeidsmarktbeleid\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"CAO wage-setting, unions, loonstarheid, and labor market policy in the Dutch institutional context.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A CAO (collectieve arbeidsovereenkomst) sets wages for all workers in the metal industry at 10% above the equilibrium wage. Explain how this is similar to a minimum wage. What effect does it have on employment?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Name two advantages of unions for workers. Name one disadvantage.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Define loonstarheid. Explain why it can cause unemployment to persist even when the economy recovers.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"The government wants to increase employment. Compare two policies: (i) lowering the minimum wage, (ii) retraining subsidies for workers. For each, explain the mechanism and evaluate the likely effectiveness.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"In the Netherlands, wages rarely fall even in a recession. Explain this using the concepts CAO and loonstarheid.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Understand that unions and CAOs can set wages above equilibrium (similar to minimum price)\",\n        \"Define loonstarheid (wage rigidity): wages adjust slowly downward\",\n        \"Explain why wage rigidity can cause persistent unemployment\",\n        \"Compare labor market policies: wage flexibility vs active labor market policy (training)\",\n        \"Apply concepts to the Dutch institutional context (poldermodel, CAO system)\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"I04\",\n        \"I10\",\n        \"L17\",\n        \"L18\",\n        \"L19\",\n        \"L20\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"I04\",\n        \"I10\",\n        \"L17\",\n        \"L18\",\n        \"L19\",\n        \"L20\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Mostly conceptual/institutional. The link to minimum wage analysis (§2.4.2) makes the economic reasoning familiar. The new element is loonstarheid as a market imperfection. Exercise (e) connects theory to Dutch reality. One lesson comfortable.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.2.6\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"4.2.3\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"difficulty\": \"MIXED\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"placeholder\": true,\n        \"context\": \"Placeholder for 4.2.7: integrated practice across this chapter. It consolidates earlier theory and introduces no new theory.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Combine at least two earlier skills from this chapter in one exam-style context.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Consolidate and transfer the chapter skills for Gemengde opgaven: arbeidsmarkt.\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"v5_placeholder_needs_review\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Count-bearing consolidation paragraph. It introduces no new theory and needs a reviewed integration target exercise.\",\n      \"id\": \"4.2.7\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 2,\n      \"paragraph\": 7,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Gemengde opgaven: arbeidsmarkt\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"gemengde_opgaven\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": false,\n      \"record_status\": \"placeholder_needs_review\",\n      \"placeholder_reason\": \"Gemengde-opgaven target exercise must be designed as integration/transfer practice.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.2.7\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"placeholder_needs_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": null,\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.3.1\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 1,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Absolute en comparatieve voordelen\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Two countries A and B produce cars and grain. A: 100 hours/car, 10 hours/ton grain. B: 150 hours/car, 30 hours/ton grain.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Which country has an absolute advantage in cars? In grain?\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the opportunity cost of 1 car in each country (in tons of grain given up).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate the opportunity cost of 1 ton of grain in each country (in cars given up).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Which country has a comparative advantage in cars? In grain? Explain.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"A student says: 'Country A is better at everything, so trade doesn't make sense.' Explain why this is wrong using your answers.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Define absolute advantage (produce more with same resources)\",\n        \"Define comparative advantage (lowest opportunity cost)\",\n        \"Calculate opportunity costs from a production table\",\n        \"Explain that comparative advantage, not absolute advantage, determines trade patterns\",\n        \"Understand that trade benefits both countries even if one is better at everything\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A18\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [\n        \"A18\"\n      ],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Define absolute advantage (lower input requirement for the same output) vs comparative advantage (lower opportunity cost) and argue why CA — not AA — drives trade (concept-level, mastery_target understand; A18 covers the calculation but not the conceptual distinction cleanly)\",\n        \"Refute the 'better at everything so no trade' misconception using an opportunity-cost comparison (concept-level + verbal; the 'aha moment' exam skill)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.6\",\n        \"A4.3\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"The opportunity cost calculation is the tricky part — students must think in terms of 'what you give up.' The counterintuitive result (B can have a comparative advantage despite being worse at everything) is why the concept is hard. One lesson is tight — allocate time for the 'aha moment.'\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.3.1\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"4.3.1\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"4.3.2\",\n      \"module\": 4,\n      \"chapter\": 3,\n      \"paragraph\": 2,\n      \"paragraph_title\": \"Ruilwinst en ruilvoet\",\n      \"difficulty\": \"MEDIUM\",\n      \"target_exercise\": {\n        \"context\": \"Countries A and B from §4.3.1 each have 3000 labor hours, split evenly before trade. Terms of trade proposed: 1 car = 8 tons of grain.\",\n        \"subquestions\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"a\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Calculate production of each good in each country before trade.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"b\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Now each country fully specialises. Calculate total world production of each good.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"c\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Show that world production has increased for at least one good (without decreasing the other).\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"d\",\n            \"prompt\": \"Country A exports grain to B and imports cars. The terms of trade are: 1 car = 8 tons of grain. With trade, A consumes 15 cars and 185 tons of grain. Is A better off than before trade? Show with numbers.\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"label\": \"e\",\n            \"prompt\": \"At which terms of trade would A refuse to trade? At which would B refuse? (Hint: use opportunity costs from §4.3.1.)\"\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"lesson_goals\": [\n        \"Calculate production before and after specialisation\",\n        \"Show that specialisation increases total world output\",\n        \"Define terms of trade: the exchange ratio between goods\",\n        \"Determine the range of mutually beneficial terms of trade (between the two opportunity costs)\",\n        \"Understand that both countries can gain from trade\"\n      ],\n      \"required_skills\": [\n        \"A18\"\n      ],\n      \"prior_knowledge_assumed\": [\n        \"A18\"\n      ],\n      \"new_skills_introduced\": [],\n      \"missing_units_flagged\": [\n        \"Compute pre-trade and post-specialisation production for each country and quantify the world-output gain (A-domain candidate)\",\n        \"Define terms-of-trade as the exchange ratio between two goods and compare consumption bundles before vs after trade (concept-level + reken)\",\n        \"Determine the mutually-beneficial range of terms-of-trade from the two countries' opportunity costs (concept-level; the bounded-interval reasoning)\"\n      ],\n      \"exam_codes\": [\n        \"A2.6\",\n        \"A4.3\"\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_notes\": \"Lots of arithmetic but conceptually straightforward once comparative advantage is understood. Exercise (e) requires connecting back to opportunity costs — elegant but potentially confusing. One lesson works if §4.3.1 was solid. If not, this spills.\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"references/owned/course-blueprint-v5.md §4.3.2\",\n      \"paragraph_kind\": \"theory\",\n      \"introduces_new_theory\": true,\n      \"record_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n      \"v5_migration\": {\n        \"source_status\": \"migrated_from_v4_needs_v5_review\",\n        \"from_v4_id\": \"4.3.2\",\n        \"related_v4_ids\": [],\n        \"review_required_before_final\": true\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n",
